-- V479: Seed 130 Class 12 Biology Mock Tests (104 Real-Format 70M Papers + 26 Chapter-Wise 25M Mocks)

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_class_id UUID;
BEGIN
    SELECT id INTO v_class_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE slug IN ('class-12', 'cbse-class-12') LIMIT 1;
    IF v_class_id IS NULL THEN
        SELECT id INTO v_class_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE name ILIKE '%Class 12%' LIMIT 1;
    END IF;

    IF v_class_id IS NOT NULL THEN
        DELETE FROM learn_test_questions WHERE mock_test_id IN (SELECT id FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '12030000-%');
        DELETE FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '12030000-%';

        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

Reason (R): Wind pollination is non-directional, so feathery exposed stigmas and vast light pollen compensate for high pollen loss.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 1, 'Classic anemophily syndrome.', NULL, NULL, 'Classic anemophily syndrome.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000007', 'How does Filiform Apparatus inside synergids guide fertilisation?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 2, 'Cellular thickenings at the micropylar tip of synergids secrete chemotropic cues guiding pollen tube entry into the embryo sac.', '2M', NULL, 'Cellular thickenings at the micropylar tip of synergids secrete chemotropic cues guiding pollen tube entry into the embryo sac.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000008', 'Distinguish between Geitonogamy and Xenogamy.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 2, 'Geitonogamy: pollen from anther to stigma of another flower on SAME plant (functionally cross, genetically self). Xenogamy: pollen to different plant (genetically cross).', '1M each', NULL, 'Geitonogamy: pollen from anther to stigma of another flower on SAME plant (functionally cross, genetically self). Xenogamy: pollen to different plant (genetically cross).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000009', 'Explain three outbreeding devices developed by flowering plants to discourage self-pollination and prevent inbreeding depression.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 3, '(1) Non-synchronised pollen release & stigma receptivity (Dichogamy). (2) Anther & stigma at different positions (Heterostyly). (3) Self-incompatibility (genetic prevention of self-pollen germination) or Unisexuality (dioecious Papaya / monoecious Maize prevents autogamy).', '3 × 1M', NULL, '(1) Non-synchronised pollen release & stigma receptivity (Dichogamy). (2) Anther & stigma at different positions (Heterostyly). (3) Self-incompatibility (genetic prevention of self-pollen germination) or Unisexuality (dioecious Papaya / monoecious Maize prevents autogamy).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', 'Why is Apple/Strawberry/Cashew called a "False Fruit" while Banana is "Parthenocarpic"? Explain Apomixis and Polyembryony in Citrus/Mango.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 3, 'False fruit develops with thalamus participation; Parthenocarpic forms without fertilisation (seedless). Apomixis mimics sexual reproduction producing seeds without fertilisation (maintains hybrid vigour without costly annual hybrid seed buying); Polyembryony = >1 embryo per seed.', '1M × 3', NULL, 'False fruit develops with thalamus participation; Parthenocarpic forms without fertilisation (seedless). Apomixis mimics sexual reproduction producing seeds without fertilisation (maintains hybrid vigour without costly annual hybrid seed buying); Polyembryony = >1 embryo per seed.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b', 'Trace Microsporogenesis & Pollen grain (2-celled vs 3-celled shedding) OR trace Double Fertilisation in angiosperms (syngamy + triple fusion forming 3n Primary Endosperm Nucleus) and explain why endosperm development precedes embryo development.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 5, 'Double fertilisation steps cleanly explained (3M): 2 male gametes released into synergid -> 1 fuses with egg (2n zygote) + 1 fuses with 2 polar nuclei (3n PEN). Endosperm precedes embryo to guarantee immediate nutritive tissue for the dividing zygote; contrast free-nuclear vs cellular endosperm in coconut (2M).', 'Double fertilisation (3M) + Endosperm primacy & coconut (2M)', NULL, 'Double fertilisation steps cleanly explained (3M): 2 male gametes released into synergid -> 1 fuses with egg (2n zygote) + 1 fuses with 2 polar nuclei (3n PEN). Endosperm precedes embryo to guarantee immediate nutritive tissue for the dividing zygote; contrast free-nuclear vs cellular endosperm in coconut (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c', '(i) Steps of Artificial Hybridisation (Emasculation & Bagging) on a bisexual flower. (ii) Which step is skipped if female parent bears unisexual flowers? (iii) Number of meiotic divisions needed to form 100 pollen grains vs 100 eggs. (iv) Contrast dormancy vs viability in Lupinus arcticus and Phoenix dactylifera.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 4, '(i) Remove anthers before dehiscence (emasculation) -> cover with butter-paper bag -> dust desired pollen on receptive stigma -> rebag. (ii) Emasculation skipped in unisexual female flowers. (iii) 25 meiosis for 100 pollen; 100 meiosis for 100 functional eggs. (iv) Lupinus 10,000 yrs; Date palm 2,000 yrs.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Plant breeders at an agricultural research station produce high-yielding F₁ wheat hybrids by controlling pollen parents through precise emasculation and bagging.', '(i) Remove anthers before dehiscence (emasculation) -> cover with butter-paper bag -> dust desired pollen on receptive stigma -> rebag. (ii) Emasculation skipped in unisexual female flowers. (iii) 25 meiosis for 100 pollen; 100 meiosis for 100 functional eggs. (iv) Lupinus 10,000 yrs; Date palm 2,000 yrs.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d', 'Acrosome cap of human sperm containing hydrolytic enzymes (hyaluronidase, acrosin) is derived from:', '{"A":"Mitochondria","B":"Golgi apparatus","C":"Centriole","D":"Lysosome only"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 1, 'Modified Golgi vesicle aids zona pellucida penetration.', NULL, NULL, 'Modified Golgi vesicle aids zona pellucida penetration.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', 'Surge of which pituitary hormone around Day 14 triggers rupture of Graafian follicle (Ovulation)?', '{"A":"FSH","B":"LH (Luteinising Hormone surge)","C":"Progesterone","D":"Oxytocin"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 1, 'Mid-cycle LH surge induces ovulation and corpus luteum formation.', NULL, NULL, 'Mid-cycle LH surge induces ovulation and corpus luteum formation.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000f', 'Part of the fallopian tube closest to the ovary bearing finger-like fimbriae is:', '{"A":"Isthmus","B":"Ampulla","C":"Infundibulum","D":"Cervix"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 1, 'Funnel-shaped infundibulum collects ovulated secondary oocyte; fertilisation occurs at ampullary region.', NULL, NULL, 'Funnel-shaped infundibulum collects ovulated secondary oocyte; fertilisation occurs at ampullary region.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', 'Which embryonic cell layer gives rise to all tissues and organs via stem cells inside the blastocyst?', '{"A":"Trophoblast","B":"Inner Cell Mass (Embryoblast)","C":"Zona pellucida","D":"Corona radiata"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 1, 'Trophoblast implants into endometrium; Inner cell mass forms embryo proper.', NULL, NULL, 'Trophoblast implants into endometrium; Inner cell mass forms embryo proper.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000011', 'Which hormone secreted ONLY during pregnancy (by placenta) is detected in home urine pregnancy kits?', '{"A":"Progesterone","B":"hCG (Human Chorionic Gonadotropin)","C":"Prolactin","D":"Estrogen"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 1, 'hCG (alongside hPL and Relaxin) is strictly pregnancy-specific.', NULL, NULL, 'hCG (alongside hPL and Relaxin) is strictly pregnancy-specific.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000012', 'Assertion (A): Presence or absence of hymen is NOT a reliable indicator of virginity or sexual experience.
Reason (R): Hymen is a thin membrane that can be torn easily by sports, cycling, horseback riding, or sudden jolt, or may persist even after intercourse.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 1, 'Important clinical/NCERT clarification.', NULL, NULL, 'Important clinical/NCERT clarification.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000013', 'State exact functions of Leydig cells, Sertoli cells, and Bulbourethral (Cowper''s) glands in human males.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 2, 'Leydig: secrete androgens (testosterone). Sertoli: nourish germ cells inside seminiferous tubules. Bulbourethral: lubricate penis.', '2M', NULL, 'Leydig: secrete androgens (testosterone). Sertoli: nourish germ cells inside seminiferous tubules. Bulbourethral: lubricate penis.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', 'Why is the first yellowish milk ("Colostrum") produced right after childbirth essential for newborns?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 2, 'Rich in IgA antibodies providing vital passive immunity protecting infants against neonatal infections.', 'IgA passive immunity (2M)', NULL, 'Rich in IgA antibodies providing vital passive immunity protecting infants against neonatal infections.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'Compare Spermatogenesis vs Oogenesis on: Onset age, Number of functional gametes per primary cell, Arrest stages, and Polar body formation.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 3, 'Spermatogenesis: puberty, 4 equal sperms/primary spermatocyte, no meiotic arrest, no polar bodies. Oogenesis: embryonic stage, 1 ovum/primary oocyte, arrests at Diplotene (Prophase I) & Metaphase II, forms unequal polar bodies.', '3M', NULL, 'Spermatogenesis: puberty, 4 equal sperms/primary spermatocyte, no meiotic arrest, no polar bodies. Oogenesis: embryonic stage, 1 ovum/primary oocyte, arrests at Diplotene (Prophase I) & Metaphase II, forms unequal polar bodies.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', 'Explain the Foetal Ejection Reflex and role of Oxytocin during Parturition.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 3, 'Mild uterine contractions triggered by fully developed foetus & placenta -> signal maternal neurohypophysis -> releases Oxytocin -> stronger uterine smooth muscle contractions -> positive feedback reflex until baby expulsion.', '3M', NULL, 'Mild uterine contractions triggered by fully developed foetus & placenta -> signal maternal neurohypophysis -> releases Oxytocin -> stronger uterine smooth muscle contractions -> positive feedback reflex until baby expulsion.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'Explain the four phases of the Menstrual Cycle (Menstrual, Follicular/Proliferative, Ovulatory, Luteal/Secretory) correlating Pituitary hormones (FSH, LH) and Ovarian hormones (Estrogen, Progesterone) with uterine endometrial changes across Days 1–28.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 5, '4 phases traced (3M) + Hormonal interplay: FSH/LH stimulate follicle -> Estrogen rebuilds endometrium -> Day 14 LH surge ovulates -> Corpus luteum Progesterone thickens secretory lining, degenerating if unfertilised causing menstruation (2M).', '4 phases (3M) + Hormone graph logic (2M)', NULL, '4 phases traced (3M) + Hormonal interplay: FSH/LH stimulate follicle -> Estrogen rebuilds endometrium -> Day 14 LH surge ovulates -> Corpus luteum Progesterone thickens secretory lining, degenerating if unfertilised causing menstruation (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', '(i) Pathway of sperm from Seminiferous tubules to Urethral meatus. (ii) Composition of seminal plasma. (iii) Chromosome count in Spermatogonia, Primary spermatocyte, Secondary spermatocyte, and Spermatid. (iv) Role of Zona pellucida in preventing polyspermy.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 4, '(i) Seminiferous tubules -> Rete testis -> Vasa efferentia -> Epididymis -> Vas deferens -> Ejaculatory duct -> Urethra. (ii) Fructose, calcium, certain enzymes (from seminal vesicles, prostate, bulbourethral). (iii) 46, 46, 23, 23. (iv) Sperm binding induces depolarisation/cortical changes blocking additional sperms.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Reproductive endocrinologists track LH surges, corpus luteum progesterone plateaus, and blastocyst implantation markers during fertility evaluations.', '(i) Seminiferous tubules -> Rete testis -> Vasa efferentia -> Epididymis -> Vas deferens -> Ejaculatory duct -> Urethra. (ii) Fructose, calcium, certain enzymes (from seminal vesicles, prostate, bulbourethral). (iii) 46, 46, 23, 23. (iv) Sperm binding induces depolarisation/cortical changes blocking additional sperms.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'Amniocentesis, legally banned for foetal sex determination in India, was originally meant to detect:', '{"A":"Twins","B":"Chromosomal genetic disorders (Down syndrome, haemophilia, sickle-cell anaemia) via amniotic cells","C":"Blood group only","D":"Heart rate"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 1, 'Chromosomal karyotype test of foetal fluid cells.', NULL, NULL, 'Chromosomal karyotype test of foetal fluid cells.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', '"Saheli", the non-steroidal once-a-week oral contraceptive pill developed at CDRI Lucknow, contains:', '{"A":"Estrogen + Progesterone","B":"Centchroman (selective estrogen receptor modulator)","C":"Copper ions","D":"Testosterone"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 1, 'High contraceptive value with minimal side effects.', NULL, NULL, 'High contraceptive value with minimal side effects.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'Which copper-releasing vs hormone-releasing IUDs suppress sperm motility/fertilising capacity vs make uterus unsuitable for implantation?', '{"A":"Lippes loop and Condom","B":"CuT / Cu7 / Multiload 375 and Progestasert / LNG-20","C":"Saheli and Tubectomy","D":"Diaphragm and Vaults"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 1, 'Medicated Cu IUDs vs hormone-releasing IUDs.', NULL, NULL, 'Medicated Cu IUDs vs hormone-releasing IUDs.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'MTP (Medical Termination of Pregnancy) is considered relatively safest during:', '{"A":"First trimester (up to 12 weeks)","B":"Third trimester","C":"28th week","D":"Anytime without supervision"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 1, 'Much safer in 1st trimester; amended Act restricts later terminations cleanly.', NULL, NULL, 'Much safer in 1st trimester; amended Act restricts later terminations cleanly.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'In Assisted Reproductive Technology, transferring an embryo with >8 blastomeres directly into the uterus is termed:', '{"A":"ZIFT","B":"IUT (Intra Uterine Transfer)","C":"GIFT","D":"ICSI"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 1, 'Up to 8 blastomeres -> ZIFT (fallopian tube); >8 blastomeres -> IUT (uterus).', NULL, NULL, 'Up to 8 blastomeres -> ZIFT (fallopian tube); >8 blastomeres -> IUT (uterus).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'Assertion (A): Lactational amenorrhea (absence of ovulation during intense breastfeeding) acts as an effective natural contraceptive only up to ~6 months post-parturition.
Reason (R): High prolactin levels during intense nursing suppress GnRH/LH/FSH ovulation transiently for about six months without side effects.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 1, 'Prolactin-mediated ovulatory suppression.', NULL, NULL, 'Prolactin-mediated ovulatory suppression.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'Contrast ZIFT and GIFT Assisted Reproductive Technologies.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 2, 'ZIFT (Zygote Intra Fallopian Transfer): in-vitro fertilised zygote/early embryo (<8 blastomeres) placed into fallopian tube. GIFT (Gamete Intra Fallopian Transfer): washed ovum from donor transferred into fallopian tube of female unable to produce ova.', '1M each', NULL, 'ZIFT (Zygote Intra Fallopian Transfer): in-vitro fertilised zygote/early embryo (<8 blastomeres) placed into fallopian tube. GIFT (Gamete Intra Fallopian Transfer): washed ovum from donor transferred into fallopian tube of female unable to produce ova.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'Why is early detection of STIs (like Chlamydiasis, Gonorrhoea, Genital Herpes) critical despite initial asymptomatic mild itching/discharge?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 2, 'Delayed treatment leads to Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID), abortions, stillbirths, ectopic pregnancies, infertility, or reproductive tract cancer.', 'Complications listed (2M)', NULL, 'Delayed treatment leads to Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID), abortions, stillbirths, ectopic pregnancies, infertility, or reproductive tract cancer.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', 'Explain the three categories of Intra Uterine Devices (IUDs) with one example and exact mechanism of action for each.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 3, '(1) Non-medicated (Lippes loop: increases phagocytosis of sperms). (2) Copper releasing (CuT, Cu7, Multiload 375: Cu ions suppress sperm motility & fertilising capacity). (3) Hormone releasing (Progestasert, LNG-20: make uterus hostile & cervix hostile to sperms).', '1M × 3', NULL, '(1) Non-medicated (Lippes loop: increases phagocytosis of sperms). (2) Copper releasing (CuT, Cu7, Multiload 375: Cu ions suppress sperm motility & fertilising capacity). (3) Hormone releasing (Progestasert, LNG-20: make uterus hostile & cervix hostile to sperms).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', 'Explain ICSI (Intra Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection) and AI / IUI (Artificial Insemination / Intrauterine Insemination) for male-factor infertility.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 3, 'ICSI: single sperm directly injected into ovum cytoplasm in lab (very low sperm count). AI/IUI: semen collected from husband/healthy donor artificially introduced into vagina or uterus (low sperm count or insemination inability).', '1.5M each', NULL, 'ICSI: single sperm directly injected into ovum cytoplasm in lab (very low sperm count). AI/IUI: semen collected from husband/healthy donor artificially introduced into vagina or uterus (low sperm count or insemination inability).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'Discuss five major strategies/measures to control rapid population explosion and ensure Maternal-Child Reproductive Health in India (awareness, statutory marriage age, spacing contraceptives, surgical Vasectomy/Tubectomy, emergency progestogen pills within 72h). Describe why Hepatitis-B, Genital Herpes, and HIV are NOT completely curable unlike bacterial STIs.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 5, '5 population/health strategies (3.5M) + Viral STIs integration/non-curability (1.5M).', 'Strategies (3.5M) + Viral STIs (1.5M)', NULL, '5 population/health strategies (3.5M) + Viral STIs integration/non-curability (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', '(i) Expand RCH programmes and MMR/IMR. (ii) Which contraceptive method simultaneously protects against STIs/AIDS? (iii) Mechanism of combined oral progestogen-estrogen pills taken 21 days starting first 5 days of menses. (iv) Contrast Vasectomy vs Tubectomy surgical procedures.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 4, '(i) Reproductive and Child Health; Maternal Mortality Rate / Infant Mortality Rate. (ii) Condoms (barrier method). (iii) Inhibit ovulation & implantation + alter cervical mucus blocking sperm entry. (iv) Cutting/tying small part of vas deferens (scrotal) vs fallopian tubes (abdomen/vagina).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A community health clinic counsels young couples on reversible barrier, IUD, and hormonal spacing options alongside screening against asymptomatic viral infections.', '(i) Reproductive and Child Health; Maternal Mortality Rate / Infant Mortality Rate. (ii) Condoms (barrier method). (iii) Inhibit ovulation & implantation + alter cervical mucus blocking sperm entry. (iv) Cutting/tying small part of vas deferens (scrotal) vs fallopian tubes (abdomen/vagina).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'When F₁ progeny is crossed back with its recessive parent (Tt × tt), the ratio obtained in this Test Cross is:', '{"A":"3 : 1","B":"1 : 1","C":"9 : 3 : 3 : 1","D":"1 : 2 : 1"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 1, 'Used by Mendel to determine heterozygosity vs homozygosity of dominant phenotype.', NULL, NULL, 'Used by Mendel to determine heterozygosity vs homozygosity of dominant phenotype.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'Incomplete dominance in Snapdragon (Antirrhinum majus) shows F₂ phenotypic and genotypic ratio:', '{"A":"3 : 1 and 1 : 2 : 1","B":"Both 1 : 2 : 1 (1 Red : 2 Pink : 1 White)","C":"9 : 3 : 3 : 1","D":"2 : 1"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 1, 'Pink F₁ (Rr) selfed yields identical 1:2:1 phenotypic & genotypic ratio.', NULL, NULL, 'Pink F₁ (Rr) selfed yields identical 1:2:1 phenotypic & genotypic ratio.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000027', 'ABO blood grouping in humans is a classic example of:', '{"A":"Pleiotropy only","B":"Co-dominance (I^A I^B expresses both sugars) AND Multiple allelism (I^A, I^B, i)","C":"Polygenic inheritance only","D":"Linkage"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 1, '3 alleles in population give 6 genotypes and 4 phenotypes.', NULL, NULL, '3 alleles in population give 6 genotypes and 4 phenotypes.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000028', 'In Honeybee (Haplo-diploid sex determination), males (drones) develop via parthenogenesis and have:', '{"A":"32 chromosomes","B":"16 chromosomes (haploid) and produce sperms by MITOSIS (no fathers, can have grandsons)","C":"XY chromosomes","D":"ZW chromosomes"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 1, 'Female worker/queen = 32 (diploid); drone = 16 (haploid).', NULL, NULL, 'Female worker/queen = 32 (diploid); drone = 16 (haploid).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000029', 'Trisomy of Chromosome 21 (47, XX/XY +21) resulting in short stature, furrowed tongue, and palm crease is:', '{"A":"Klinefelter''s syndrome","B":"Down''s syndrome","C":"Turner''s syndrome","D":"Phenylketonuria"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 1, 'Autosomal aneuploidy first described by Langdon Down (1866).', NULL, NULL, 'Autosomal aneuploidy first described by Langdon Down (1866).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002a', 'Assertion (A): T.H. Morgan selected Drosophila melanogaster (fruit fly) and discovered Linkage & Recombination.
Reason (R): Two genes located very tightly on the same chromosome show minimal crossing over and stay together far above expected 9:3:3:1 independent assortment.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 1, 'Physical proximity lowers recombination frequency (< 50%).', NULL, NULL, 'Physical proximity lowers recombination frequency (< 50%).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002b', 'Distinguish between Pleiotropy and Polygenic Inheritance with one human example each.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 2, 'Pleiotropy: single gene controls multiple traits (Phenylketonuria: mental retardation + hair/skin pigment loss). Polygenic: multiple genes additively control one continuous trait (Human skin colour A,B,C or height).', '1M each', NULL, 'Pleiotropy: single gene controls multiple traits (Phenylketonuria: mental retardation + hair/skin pigment loss). Polygenic: multiple genes additively control one continuous trait (Human skin colour A,B,C or height).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002c', 'Compare Chromosomal karyotype & symptoms of Klinefelter''s syndrome vs Turner''s syndrome.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 2, 'Klinefelter (47, XXY): sterile tall male with gynaecomastia. Turner (45, XO): sterile short female with rudimentary ovaries & lack of secondary sexual traits.', '1M each', NULL, 'Klinefelter (47, XXY): sterile tall male with gynaecomastia. Turner (45, XO): sterile short female with rudimentary ovaries & lack of secondary sexual traits.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002d', 'Explain why Haemophilia and Colour Blindness (X-linked recessive) affect males far more frequently than females. Trace how a carrier mother and normal father pass colour blindness to children.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 3, 'Males are hemizygous (XY), so a single mutant X expresses the disorder; females need two mutant X''s (rare). Carrier X^C X × XY -> 50% sons colour blind (X^C Y), 50% daughters carriers (X^C X).', 'Reason (1.5M) + Cross (1.5M)', NULL, 'Males are hemizygous (XY), so a single mutant X expresses the disorder; females need two mutant X''s (rare). Carrier X^C X × XY -> 50% sons colour blind (X^C Y), 50% daughters carriers (X^C X).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002e', 'Distinguish between α-Thalassemia and β-Thalassemia (controlled genes and chromosome numbers), and contrast Sickle-cell anaemia (qualitative globin change) vs Thalassemia (quantitative reduction).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 3, 'α-Thalassemia: HBA1, HBA2 on Chr 16 (4 linked alleles). β-Thalassemia: HBB on Chr 11. Sickle-cell = qualitative Val-for-Glu point mutation; Thalassemia = quantitative too few globin chains.', 'Genes & chromosomes (1.5M) + Qual vs Quant (1.5M)', NULL, 'α-Thalassemia: HBA1, HBA2 on Chr 16 (4 linked alleles). β-Thalassemia: HBB on Chr 11. Sickle-cell = qualitative Val-for-Glu point mutation; Thalassemia = quantitative too few globin chains.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002f', 'Explain Chromosomal Theory of Inheritance (Sutton & Boveri) paralleling chromosomes and Mendelian genes. Describe Morgan''s dihybrid crosses on Drosophila (Cross A: white/yellow tightly linked 1.3% recombination vs Cross B: white/miniature 37.2% recombination), showing how Alfred Sturtevant constructed genetic maps from % recombination.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 5, 'Sutton-Boveri parallels (2M) + Morgan Linkage crosses A & B (2M) + Sturtevant mapping 1% = 1 cM (1M).', 'Theory (2M) + Morgan crosses (2M) + Mapping (1M)', NULL, 'Sutton-Boveri parallels (2M) + Morgan Linkage crosses A & B (2M) + Sturtevant mapping 1% = 1 cM (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', '(i) Identify sex determination mechanism in Birds (ZW-ZZ) vs Grasshoppers (XO-XX). (ii) Which parent determines sex of baby in birds? (iii) Identify pedigree symbol: shaded circle vs square with horizontal line to circle + consanguineous double line. (iv) Amino acid change at 6th position of β-globin in Sickle-cell anaemia (GAG -> GUG).', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', 4, '(i) Birds: female heterogamety (ZW female, ZZ male); Grasshopper: XO male, XX female. (ii) Female bird (lays Z or W ovum). (iii) Affected female; marriage vs consanguineous marriage between close relatives. (iv) Glutamic acid replaced by Valine.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A genetics clinic interprets family pedigree charts for X-linked clotting disorders alongside autosomal recessive sickle-cell haemoglobin mutations.', '(i) Birds: female heterogamety (ZW female, ZZ male); Grasshopper: XO male, XX female. (ii) Female bird (lays Z or W ovum). (iii) Affected female; marriage vs consanguineous marriage between close relatives. (iv) Glutamic acid replaced by Valine.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000031', 'Which experiment by Hershey & Chase (1952) using ³²P and ³⁵S bacteriophages proved unequivocally that DNA is the genetic material?', '{"A":"Griffith''s mouse transformation","B":"Blending/centrifugation showing radioactive ³²P DNA entered E. coli cells while ³⁵S protein coats remained outside","C":"Meselson-Stahl density gradient","D":"Avery-MacLeod-McCarty"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 1, '³²P labels DNA backbone; ³⁵S labels capsid amino acids (Cys/Met).', NULL, NULL, '³²P labels DNA backbone; ³⁵S labels capsid amino acids (Cys/Met).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000032', 'Why does one DNA strand replicate continuously (5''->3'') while the other replicates discontinuously as Okazaki fragments?', '{"A":"DNA helicase stops","B":"DNA polymerase III can polymerise nucleotides ONLY in 5'' -> 3'' direction","C":"RNA primer blocks it","D":"Ligase fails"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 1, 'Antiparallel strands + unidirectional 5''->3'' polymerase impose lagging Okazaki fragments.', NULL, NULL, 'Antiparallel strands + unidirectional 5''->3'' polymerase impose lagging Okazaki fragments.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000033', 'In Eukaryotes, primary transcript hnRNA undergoes three processing steps inside nucleus before becoming mRNA:', '{"A":"Replication, Ligation, Restriction","B":"Capping (mGppp at 5''), Splicing (introns removed, exons joined), Tailing (poly-A at 3'')","C":"Methylation only","D":"Translation"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 1, 'Protects mRNA and eliminates non-coding introns.', NULL, NULL, 'Protects mRNA and eliminates non-coding introns.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000034', 'The Start Codon AUG codes for Methionine and exhibits dual function; the three Stop (Nonsense) codons are:', '{"A":"UAA, UAG, UGA","B":"AUG, GUG, UGG","C":"UUU, CCC, AAA","D":"UUA, UGG, UGA"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 1, 'UAA, UAG, UGA lack tRNAs and bind release factors.', NULL, NULL, 'UAA, UAG, UGA lack tRNAs and bind release factors.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000035', 'In the Lac Operon of E. coli, allolactose/lactose acts as the:', '{"A":"Repressor protein","B":"Inducer that binds and inactivates the Lac repressor, allowing RNA polymerase transcription of lacZ, lacY, lacA","C":"Co-repressor","D":"Terminator"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 1, 'Negative inducible operon turns ON when lactose is present.', NULL, NULL, 'Negative inducible operon turns ON when lactose is present.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000036', 'Assertion (A): DNA is chemically more stable and preferred over RNA for genetic information storage across most organisms.
Reason (R): DNA contains 2''-deoxyribose (lacking reactive 2''-OH group present in RNA), Thymine instead of Uracil, and double-stranded repair backup.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 1, 'Deoxyribose + Thymine = superior chemical stability.', NULL, NULL, 'Deoxyribose + Thymine = superior chemical stability.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000037', 'List the three enzymatic products encoded by structural genes lac z, lac y, and lac a in the Lac Operon.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 2, 'lac z: β-galactosidase (lactose -> glucose + galactose); lac y: permease (increases cell permeability to β-galactosides); lac a: transacetylase.', '0.75M+0.75M+0.5M', NULL, 'lac z: β-galactosidase (lactose -> glucose + galactose); lac y: permease (increases cell permeability to β-galactosides); lac a: transacetylase.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000038', 'State four salient features of the Genetic Code (Triplet, Unambiguous, Degenerate, Universal).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 2, 'Codon is triplet (61 sense + 3 stop), unambiguous (1 codon = 1 amino acid), degenerate (most amino acids have >1 codon), comma-less & nearly universal across organisms.', '0.5M × 4', NULL, 'Codon is triplet (61 sense + 3 stop), unambiguous (1 codon = 1 amino acid), degenerate (most amino acids have >1 codon), comma-less & nearly universal across organisms.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000039', 'Describe Meselson and Stahl''s 1958 heavy nitrogen (¹⁵N / ¹⁴N CsCl density gradient) experiment proving Semiconservative DNA Replication.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 3, 'Grew E. coli in ¹⁵NH₄Cl (heavy band), shifted to ¹⁴NH₄Cl: After Gen I (20 min) single intermediate hybrid band (¹⁵N/¹⁴N); After Gen II (40 min) equal 50% hybrid + 50% light ¹⁴N bands -> proved each daughter retains 1 parental strand.', 'Experimental steps (2M) + Band ratios (1M)', NULL, 'Grew E. coli in ¹⁵NH₄Cl (heavy band), shifted to ¹⁴NH₄Cl: After Gen I (20 min) single intermediate hybrid band (¹⁵N/¹⁴N); After Gen II (40 min) equal 50% hybrid + 50% light ¹⁴N bands -> proved each daughter retains 1 parental strand.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003a', 'Explain the structure of a Nucleosome in eukaryotic chromatin packaging and distinguish Euchromatin vs Heterochromatin.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 3, 'Negatively charged DNA (~200 bp) wraps around positively charged histone octamer (H2A,H2B,H3,H4 ×2 + H1 linker). Euchromatin: loosely packed, light-staining, transcriptionally active; Heterochromatin: densely packed, dark, inactive.', 'Nucleosome (1.5M) + Eu/Hetero (1.5M)', NULL, 'Negatively charged DNA (~200 bp) wraps around positively charged histone octamer (H2A,H2B,H3,H4 ×2 + H1 linker). Euchromatin: loosely packed, light-staining, transcriptionally active; Heterochromatin: densely packed, dark, inactive.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003b', 'Trace Eukaryotic Transcription (3 RNA polymerases I/II/III, Promoter, Structural gene, Terminator; Capping, Splicing, Tailing) OR explain DNA Fingerprinting (Alec Jeffreys) step-by-step from DNA isolation -> Restriction digestion -> Electrophoresis -> Southern Blotting -> VNTR probe hybridisation -> Autoradiography, citing two forensic/paternity applications.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 5, 'Stepwise DNA Fingerprinting (isolation, digestion, electrophoresis, nylon Southern blot, radioactive VNTR probe, X-ray bands) (3.5M) + VNTR polymorphism principle & forensic/paternity uses (1.5M).', 'Steps (3.5M) + Principle & uses (1.5M)', NULL, 'Stepwise DNA Fingerprinting (isolation, digestion, electrophoresis, nylon Southern blot, radioactive VNTR probe, X-ray bands) (3.5M) + VNTR polymorphism principle & forensic/paternity uses (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003c', '(i) Write mRNA sequence transcribed from template DNA strand 3''-T A C G T A C G A T T-5''. (ii) Role of Adapter molecule (tRNA) cloverleaf anticodon loop. (iii) Number of base pairs in haploid human genome & two Human Genome Project (HGP) goals. (iv) Contrast Cistron, Exons, and Introns.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 4, '(i) 5''-A U G C A U G C U A A-3''. (ii) Carries specific amino acid at 3'' CCA end and reads mRNA codons via anticodon base pairing. (iii) ~3.3 × 10⁹ bp; identify ~20,000–25,000 human genes & store sequence data / address ELSI. (iv) Cistron: DNA segment coding a polypeptide; Exons: expressed coding sequences; Introns: intervening sequences spliced out.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Forensic DNA labs amplify polymorphic VNTR microsatellite repeats from trace blood cells to resolve paternity disputes and verify genetic kinship cleanly.', '(i) 5''-A U G C A U G C U A A-3''. (ii) Carries specific amino acid at 3'' CCA end and reads mRNA codons via anticodon base pairing. (iii) ~3.3 × 10⁹ bp; identify ~20,000–25,000 human genes & store sequence data / address ELSI. (iv) Cistron: DNA segment coding a polypeptide; Exons: expressed coding sequences; Introns: intervening sequences spliced out.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003d', 'Miller and Urey (1953) simulated primitive Earth''s reducing atmosphere in a spark-discharge flask at 800°C containing:', '{"A":"O₂, N₂, CO₂, H₂O","B":"CH₄, NH₃, H₂, and Water vapour (producing amino acids)","C":"O₃, CH₄, He","D":"SO₂, O₂, NH₃"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 1, 'Zero free O₂ reducing atmosphere yielded amino acids like alanine, glycine, aspartic acid.', NULL, NULL, 'Zero free O₂ reducing atmosphere yielded amino acids like alanine, glycine, aspartic acid.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003e', 'Thorn of Bougainvillea and Tendril of Cucurbita vs Wings of Butterfly and Wings of Birds respectively represent:', '{"A":"Both analogous","B":"Homologous organs (divergent evolution) and Analogous organs (convergent evolution)","C":"Both vestigial","D":"Atavism"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 1, 'Same axillary bud origin = homologous; different origin same flight function = analogous.', NULL, NULL, 'Same axillary bud origin = homologous; different origin same flight function = analogous.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003f', 'Darwin''s Finches on Galapagos Islands and Australian Marsupials illustrate:', '{"A":"Genetic drift","B":"Adaptive Radiation (diversifying from common ancestor into multiple ecological niches)","C":"Saltation","D":"Convergent isolation"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 1, 'Beak variations evolved for distinct seed/insect/fruit diets.', NULL, NULL, 'Beak variations evolved for distinct seed/insect/fruit diets.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000040', 'Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium equation for allele frequencies p and q (p + q = 1) is:', '{"A":"p² + q² = 1","B":"p² + 2pq + q² = 1","C":"2p + 2q = 1","D":"p² - q² = 1"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 1, 'p² (AA), 2pq (Aa), q² (aa).', NULL, NULL, 'p² (AA), 2pq (Aa), q² (aa).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000041', 'Which hominid ancestor had cranial capacity ~1400 cc, used hides to protect body, and buried their dead?', '{"A":"Homo habilis (650–800 cc)","B":"Neanderthal man (1400 cc, East & Central Asia ~100,000–40,000 yrs ago)","C":"Homo erectus (900 cc)","D":"Australopithecus"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 1, 'Neanderthals buried their dead and wore hides.', NULL, NULL, 'Neanderthals buried their dead and wore hides.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000042', 'Assertion (A): Industrial melanism in Biston betularia moths around England (1850 vs 1920) demonstrates Natural Selection rather than complete species wipeout.
Reason (R): Dark melanic moths gained camouflage advantage over white-winged moths on soot-darkened lichen-free tree trunks during industrialisation.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 1, 'Directional natural selection shifted moth phenotype ratio.', NULL, NULL, 'Directional natural selection shifted moth phenotype ratio.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000043', 'What is Founder Effect in Genetic Drift? Give an example.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 2, 'When a few drifted individuals colonise a new isolated habitat, they become founders carrying a unrepresentative allele subset causing stark frequency differences in the new population.', 'Definition (1.5M) + Example (0.5M)', NULL, 'When a few drifted individuals colonise a new isolated habitat, they become founders carrying a unrepresentative allele subset causing stark frequency differences in the new population.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000044', 'Contrast Stabilising, Directional, and Disruptive Natural Selection curves briefly.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 2, 'Stabilising: peaks higher & narrower around mean (birth weight); Directional: peak shifts toward one extreme (industrial melanism); Disruptive: two peripheral peaks form.', '2M', NULL, 'Stabilising: peaks higher & narrower around mean (birth weight); Directional: peak shifts toward one extreme (industrial melanism); Disruptive: two peripheral peaks form.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000045', 'List the five evolutionary factors known to disturb Hardy-Weinberg genetic equilibrium.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 3, 'Gene migration (gene flow), Genetic drift, Mutation, Genetic recombination, and Natural selection.', '3M', NULL, 'Gene migration (gene flow), Genetic drift, Mutation, Genetic recombination, and Natural selection.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000046', 'Contrast Charles Darwin''s Theory of Natural Selection ("branching descent" via small gradual variations) with Hugo de Vries'' Mutation Theory ("saltation").', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 3, 'Darwin: minor, continuous, directional variations over vast time. de Vries: sudden, large, random, single-step mutations (saltation) causing speciation directly.', '1.5M each', NULL, 'Darwin: minor, continuous, directional variations over vast time. de Vries: sudden, large, random, single-step mutations (saltation) causing speciation directly.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000047', 'Trace Human Evolution chronologically from Dryopithecus & Ramapithecus -> Australopithecines -> Homo habilis -> Homo erectus -> Neanderthal man -> Homo sapiens, noting cranial capacities, dietary habits (fruit vs meat), and cultural tools/cave art.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 5, '6 evolutionary stages accurately sequenced with brain volumes (650–800 cc -> 900 cc -> 1400 cc) and traits (bipedal hunting, fire/meat, hides/burial, 18,000-yr cave paintings & agriculture ~10,000 yrs ago) (5M).', 'Chronology + Brain sizes + Traits (5M)', NULL, '6 evolutionary stages accurately sequenced with brain volumes (650–800 cc -> 900 cc -> 1400 cc) and traits (bipedal hunting, fire/meat, hides/burial, 18,000-yr cave paintings & agriculture ~10,000 yrs ago) (5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000048', '(i) Convergent evolution example between placental mammals and Australian marsupials (e.g., Wolf vs Tasmanian wolf). (ii) What is "fitness" according to Darwin? (iii) In a Hardy-Weinberg population, frequency of recessive allele q = 0.4: find percentage of homozygous dominant (AA) and heterozygous carriers (Aa). (iv) Why does anthropogenic overuse of antibiotics/herbicides speed up evolution?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 4, '(i) Similar adaptive forms evolved independently in same habitats (Placental Anteater/Marsupial Numbat, Flying Squirrel/Flying Phalanger). (ii) Ultimately reproductive fitness (leaving more fertile progeny). (iii) p = 0.6 -> AA = p² = 36%; Aa = 2pq = 48%. (iv) Exerts intense selection favouring pre-existing resistant mutants within months/years rather than centuries.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Hospitals tracking MRSA antibiotic resistance and agricultural scientists studying herbicide-tolerant weeds observe textbook directional selection operating in real time.', '(i) Similar adaptive forms evolved independently in same habitats (Placental Anteater/Marsupial Numbat, Flying Squirrel/Flying Phalanger). (ii) Ultimately reproductive fitness (leaving more fertile progeny). (iii) p = 0.6 -> AA = p² = 36%; Aa = 2pq = 48%. (iv) Exerts intense selection favouring pre-existing resistant mutants within months/years rather than centuries.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000049', 'Diagnostic confirmatory test for Typhoid fever (Salmonella typhi) vs AIDS (HIV) respectively are:', '{"A":"ELISA and Widal","B":"Widal Test and ELISA (confirmed by Western Blot)","C":"Biopsy and MRI","D":"CT scan and Skin prick"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 1, 'Widal detects Typhoid antibodies; ELISA screens HIV.', NULL, NULL, 'Widal detects Typhoid antibodies; ELISA screens HIV.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004a', 'Infective stage of Plasmodium (Malaria parasite) injected into human blood by female Anopheles mosquito is:', '{"A":"Gametocyte","B":"Sporozoite (ruptures liver/RBCs releasing toxic Haemozoin causing chills & fever)","C":"Merozoite","D":"Trophozoite"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 1, 'Haemozoin release every 3–4 days triggers high fever chills.', NULL, NULL, 'Haemozoin release every 3–4 days triggers high fever chills.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004b', 'Antibodies (Immunoglobulins) are Y-shaped H₂L₂ protein molecules secreted by:', '{"A":"T-lymphocytes directly","B":"B-lymphocytes (Plasma cells) mediating Humoral Immunity","C":"Macrophages","D":"Basophils"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 1, 'T-cells mediate Cell-Mediated Immunity (CMI, graft rejection); B-cells secrete H₂L₂ antibodies.', NULL, NULL, 'T-cells mediate Cell-Mediated Immunity (CMI, graft rejection); B-cells secrete H₂L₂ antibodies.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004c', 'Colostrum IgA (mother to baby) and Tetanus anti-toxin injection both provide:', '{"A":"Active immunity","B":"Passive immunity (pre-formed ready-made antibodies)","C":"Auto-immunity","D":"Allergic reaction"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 1, 'Fast temporary protection without host antibody production.', NULL, NULL, 'Fast temporary protection without host antibody production.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004d', 'Cannabinoids (Marijuana, Hashish, Charas, Ganja) obtained from Cannabis sativa primarily affect:', '{"A":"Respiratory tract only","B":"Cardiovascular system of the body","C":"Red bone marrow","D":"Thyroid"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 1, 'Heroin (opioid) slows body functions; Cocaine stimulates CNS via dopamine blockade; Cannabinoids act on cardiovascular system.', NULL, NULL, 'Heroin (opioid) slows body functions; Cocaine stimulates CNS via dopamine blockade; Cannabinoids act on cardiovascular system.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004e', 'Assertion (A): Organ transplant recipients require lifelong immunosuppressant drugs like Cyclosporin-A.
Reason (R): Cell-mediated immunity (CMI) involving T-lymphocytes recognises transplanted non-self tissue as foreign and attacks/rejects the graft.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 1, 'T-cell CMI drives graft rejection.', NULL, NULL, 'T-cell CMI drives graft rejection.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004f', 'Distinguish between Benign Tumours and Malignant Tumours, explaining Metastasis.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 2, 'Benign: remain confined to original location, little harm. Malignant: rapidly proliferating neoplastic cells invading surrounding tissues and spreading via blood to distant organs forming secondary tumours (Metastasis).', '1M each', NULL, 'Benign: remain confined to original location, little harm. Malignant: rapidly proliferating neoplastic cells invading surrounding tissues and spreading via blood to distant organs forming secondary tumours (Metastasis).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000050', 'What causes Rheumatoid Arthritis and Allergy (naming antibody & chemicals released)?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 2, 'Rheumatoid arthritis: auto-immune disorder attacking self joint cells. Allergy: exaggerated immune response mediated by IgE antibodies triggering Histamine and Serotonin release from mast cells.', '1M each', NULL, 'Rheumatoid arthritis: auto-immune disorder attacking self joint cells. Allergy: exaggerated immune response mediated by IgE antibodies triggering Histamine and Serotonin release from mast cells.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000051', 'Explain the replication cycle of Retrovirus HIV inside a human host macrophage/Helper-T cell and explain why HIV infection steadily weakens immunity.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 3, 'Viral RNA enters macrophage -> Reverse Transcriptase makes viral DNA -> integrates into host genome -> manufactures new HIV RNA particles turning macrophages into "HIV factory". Simultaneously enters & destroys Helper T-lymphocytes (T_H), plunging count and opening fatal opportunistic infections (Mycobacterium, Toxoplasma, fungi).', 'Macrophage factory (1.5M) + Helper-T depletion (1.5M)', NULL, 'Viral RNA enters macrophage -> Reverse Transcriptase makes viral DNA -> integrates into host genome -> manufactures new HIV RNA particles turning macrophages into "HIV factory". Simultaneously enters & destroys Helper T-lymphocytes (T_H), plunging count and opening fatal opportunistic infections (Mycobacterium, Toxoplasma, fungi).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000052', 'Distinguish between Innate Immunity (4 barriers) and Acquired Immunity (Primary vs Secondary anamnestic response).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 3, 'Innate: non-specific birth barriers — Physical (skin/mucus), Physiological (stomach HCl, tears lysozyme), Cellular (PMNL neutrophils, monocytes, NK cells), Cytokine (Interferons). Acquired: pathogen-specific memory giving feeble slow primary vs intense rapid secondary anamnestic response.', '1.5M each', NULL, 'Innate: non-specific birth barriers — Physical (skin/mucus), Physiological (stomach HCl, tears lysozyme), Cellular (PMNL neutrophils, monocytes, NK cells), Cytokine (Interferons). Acquired: pathogen-specific memory giving feeble slow primary vs intense rapid secondary anamnestic response.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000053', 'Trace the complete dual-host Life Cycle of Plasmodium (Sporozoites -> Human Liver asexual -> RBC rupture releasing Haemozoin + Gametocytes -> Mosquito gut fertilisation -> Salivary gland sporozoites). Also contrast Opioid (Heroin/Smack from Papaver somniferum), Cocaine (Erythroxylum coca), and Smoking (CO & Nicotine -> adrenal adrenaline -> high BP) hazards in adolescence.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 5, 'Plasmodium life cycle cleanly (3M) + Drug/Tobacco mechanisms & adolescent prevention measures (2M).', 'Plasmodium cycle (3M) + Drug/Tobacco mechanisms (2M)', NULL, 'Plasmodium life cycle cleanly (3M) + Drug/Tobacco mechanisms & adolescent prevention measures (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000054', '(i) Vector and symptoms of Filariasis (Elephantiasis) vs Dengue/Chikungunya vector. (ii) Which lymphoid organs are Primary (maturation) vs Secondary (antigen interaction)? (iii) Why do cancer diagnostics use Biopsy, MRI, and Monoclonal Antibodies? (iv) Suggest 3 ways to curb teenage drug/alcohol abuse.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Human Health and Disease', 4, '(i) Culex mosquito (Wuchereria bancrofti chronic lower-limb lymphatic swelling); Aedes mosquito transmits Dengue & Chikungunya. (ii) Primary: Bone marrow & Thymus; Secondary: Spleen, Lymph nodes, Tonsils, Peyer''s patches, MALT (50% lymphoid tissue). (iii) Confirm malignancy histopathologically, image non-ionising 3D soft-tissue changes, detect tumour-specific antigens. (iv) Avoid undue peer pressure, counselling/sports channelisation, seeking parent/mentor help.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Public health surveillance clinics screen vector-borne fevers alongside adolescent wellness counselling and rapid ELISA viral panels.', '(i) Culex mosquito (Wuchereria bancrofti chronic lower-limb lymphatic swelling); Aedes mosquito transmits Dengue & Chikungunya. (ii) Primary: Bone marrow & Thymus; Secondary: Spleen, Lymph nodes, Tonsils, Peyer''s patches, MALT (50% lymphoid tissue). (iii) Confirm malignancy histopathologically, image non-ionising 3D soft-tissue changes, detect tumour-specific antigens. (iv) Avoid undue peer pressure, counselling/sports channelisation, seeking parent/mentor help.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000055', 'Which bacterium converts milk to curd while significantly improving Vitamin B₁₂ nutritional content?', '{"A":"Saccharomyces cerevisiae","B":"Lactobacillus (Lactic Acid Bacteria / LAB)","C":"Clostridium butylicum","D":"Acetobacter aceti"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 1, 'LAB checks disease microbes in stomach too.', NULL, NULL, 'LAB checks disease microbes in stomach too.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000056', '"Clot buster" enzyme Streptokinase used to dissolve clots in myocardial infarction heart patients comes from:', '{"A":"Trichoderma polysporum","B":"Streptococcus (genetically modified)","C":"Monascus purpureus","D":"Aspergillus niger"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 1, 'Cyclosporin-A comes from Trichoderma; Statins from Monascus purpureus.', NULL, NULL, 'Cyclosporin-A comes from Trichoderma; Statins from Monascus purpureus.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000057', 'Blood-cholesterol lowering agent "Statins" competitively inhibit cholesterol synthesis enzyme and are produced by yeast:', '{"A":"Saccharomyces","B":"Monascus purpureus","C":"Penicillium","D":"Propionibacterium"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 1, 'Statins lower blood LDL cholesterol cleanly.', NULL, NULL, 'Statins lower blood LDL cholesterol cleanly.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000058', 'Large holes in "Swiss Cheese" are caused by large CO₂ production by which bacterium?', '{"A":"Lactobacillus","B":"Propionibacterium sharmanii","C":"Aspergillus","D":"Streptomyces"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 1, 'Roquefort cheese is ripened by specific Penicillium fungus.', NULL, NULL, 'Roquefort cheese is ripened by specific Penicillium fungus.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000059', 'Which free-living nitrogen-fixing bacteria enrich soil nitrogen in non-leguminous fields?', '{"A":"Rhizobium only","B":"Azospirillum and Azotobacter","C":"Methanobacterium","D":"Glomus"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 1, 'Rhizobium is symbiotic in legume nodules; Azospirillum/Azotobacter are free-living.', NULL, NULL, 'Rhizobium is symbiotic in legume nodules; Azospirillum/Azotobacter are free-living.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005a', 'Assertion (A): Baculoviruses (genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus) are ideal species-specific narrow-spectrum biological insecticidal control agents.
Reason (R): Baculoviruses attack specific insect pests without harming mammals, birds, fish, or non-target beneficial insects, fitting Integrated Pest Management (IPM).
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 1, 'Zero non-target toxicity makes them IPM gold-standard.', NULL, NULL, 'Zero non-target toxicity makes them IPM gold-standard.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005b', 'Name the microbes commercially producing (i) Citric acid, (ii) Acetic acid, (iii) Butyric acid, and (iv) Ethanol.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 2, '(i) Aspergillus niger; (ii) Acetobacter aceti; (iii) Clostridium butylicum; (iv) Saccharomyces cerevisiae.', '0.5M × 4', NULL, '(i) Aspergillus niger; (ii) Acetobacter aceti; (iii) Clostridium butylicum; (iv) Saccharomyces cerevisiae.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005c', 'How does Fungal Mycorrhiza (genus Glomus) mutually benefit host plant roots?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 2, 'Fungal symbiont absorbs phosphorus from soil passing it to plant, confers resistance to root-borne pathogens, salinity/drought tolerance, and promotes plant growth.', 'P uptake + stress resistance (2M)', NULL, 'Fungal symbiont absorbs phosphorus from soil passing it to plant, confers resistance to root-borne pathogens, salinity/drought tolerance, and promotes plant growth.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005d', 'Distinguish between Primary Sewage Treatment (physical) and Secondary Sewage Treatment (biological), defining "Flocs" and BOD.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 3, 'Primary: sequential filtration & grit sedimentation removing floating debris & soil -> primary sludge. Secondary: aeration tanks grow vigorous aerobic bacteria + fungal filaments ("Flocs") consuming organic wastes and slashing Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) before settling as Activated Sludge.', 'Primary vs Secondary (2M) + Flocs & BOD (1M)', NULL, 'Primary: sequential filtration & grit sedimentation removing floating debris & soil -> primary sludge. Secondary: aeration tanks grow vigorous aerobic bacteria + fungal filaments ("Flocs") consuming organic wastes and slashing Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) before settling as Activated Sludge.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005e', 'Explain how Activated Sludge enters Anaerobic Sludge Digesters to produce Biogas (Gobar gas) mixture.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 3, 'Small part of activated sludge pumps back as inoculum to aeration tank; bulk enters anaerobic digesters where Methanogens (Methanobacterium) digest bacteria & fungi producing flammable CH₄ + H₂S + CO₂ biogas.', '3M', NULL, 'Small part of activated sludge pumps back as inoculum to aeration tank; bulk enters anaerobic digesters where Methanogens (Methanobacterium) digest bacteria & fungi producing flammable CH₄ + H₂S + CO₂ biogas.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005f', 'Describe the Role of Microbes in Biological Control of Pests & Diseases (Ladybird/Dragonflies, Bacillus thuringiensis Bt toxin against butterfly caterpillars, Trichoderma root fungi, Baculoviruses) AND as Biofertilisers (Rhizobium, Azotobacter, Cyanobacteria Anabaena/Nostoc/Oscillatoria in paddy fields, Mycorrhiza). Also explain why Ganga/Yamuna Action Plans stress secondary effluent treatment before river discharge.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 5, 'Biocontrol (2.5M) + Biofertilisers (2M) + River BOD protection (0.5M).', 'Biocontrol (2.5M) + Biofertilisers (2M) + River safety (0.5M)', NULL, 'Biocontrol (2.5M) + Biofertilisers (2M) + River BOD protection (0.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000060', '(i) Why did Alexander Fleming accidentally notice Penicillin inhibition zone on Staphylococci plates? (ii) Who established Penicillin''s full antibiotic potential for WWII soldiers? (iii) Enzymes used in laundry detergents vs clarifying bottled fruit juices. (iv) What does low effluent BOD indicate about water quality?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 4, '(i) Contaminating Penicillium notatum mould secreted penicillin killing surrounding Staphylococci bacteria. (ii) Ernst Chain and Howard Florey (1945 Nobel). (iii) Lipases (oily stains); Pectinases & Proteases (clear juice). (iv) Less organic pollutant load -> cleaner water with higher dissolved O₂.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Municipal sewage treatment plants and organic bio-pesticide factories rely on anaerobic methanogens, floc microbes, and Trichoderma fungi to replace harsh chemicals.', '(i) Contaminating Penicillium notatum mould secreted penicillin killing surrounding Staphylococci bacteria. (ii) Ernst Chain and Howard Florey (1945 Nobel). (iii) Lipases (oily stains); Pectinases & Proteases (clear juice). (iv) Less organic pollutant load -> cleaner water with higher dissolved O₂.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000061', 'Restriction endonuclease EcoRI cuts the palindromic DNA sequence 5''-GAATTC-3'' cleanly between:', '{"A":"A and A","B":"G and A (creating sticky overhanging ends)","C":"T and T","D":"T and C"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 1, 'Leaves 5''-AATT sticky ends easily joined by DNA ligase.', NULL, NULL, 'Leaves 5''-AATT sticky ends easily joined by DNA ligase.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000062', 'In gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments move toward the ANODE because DNA is:', '{"A":"Positively charged","B":"Negatively charged (due to phosphate sugar backbone; smaller fragments travel farthest)","C":"Neutral","D":"Lipid-soluble"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 1, 'Visualised under UV after Ethidium Bromide staining as bright orange bands.', NULL, NULL, 'Visualised under UV after Ethidium Bromide staining as bright orange bands.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000063', 'In cloning vector pBR322, inserting foreign DNA into the BamHI site inside the tetracycline resistance (tet^R) gene causes:', '{"A":"Loss of ampicillin resistance","B":"Insertional inactivation of tet^R while retaining amp^R (allowing recombinant selection)","C":"Loss of ori","D":"Cell death"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 1, 'Transformants grow on ampicillin yet fail on tetracycline.', NULL, NULL, 'Transformants grow on ampicillin yet fail on tetracycline.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000064', 'Thermostable DNA polymerase ("Taq polymerase") surviving 94°C PCR denaturation is isolated from:', '{"A":"E. coli","B":"Thermus aquaticus (hot spring bacterium)","C":"Agrobacterium","D":"Bacillus thuringiensis"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 1, 'Taq polymerase avoids refilling enzyme after each PCR heating cycle.', NULL, NULL, 'Taq polymerase avoids refilling enzyme after each PCR heating cycle.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000065', 'To break open fungal, bacterial, and plant cell walls respectively to isolate pure DNA, we treat with:', '{"A":"Lysozyme, Chitinase, Cellulase","B":"Chitinase (fungi), Lysozyme (bacteria), Cellulase (plant)","C":"Protease only","D":"RNase only"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 1, 'Chilled ethanol finally precipitates purified DNA threads ("spooling").', NULL, NULL, 'Chilled ethanol finally precipitates purified DNA threads ("spooling").', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000066', 'Assertion (A): For direct gene transfer without a vector in plant cells, the Biolistic (Gene Gun) method bombards cells with gold or tungsten microparticles coated with DNA.
Reason (R): Plant cells have rigid cell walls that resist simple heat-shock CaCl₂ uptake, whereas micro-projectiles pierce walls cleanly at high velocity.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 1, 'Gene gun suits plant transformation; Microinjection suits animal eggs.', NULL, NULL, 'Gene gun suits plant transformation; Microinjection suits animal eggs.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000067', 'What is a Palindromic Nucleotide Sequence in DNA? Write the EcoRI palindrome.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 2, 'A base sequence reading identically on both antiparallel strands in the 5'' -> 3'' direction: 5''-GAATTC-3'' / 3''-CTTAAG-5''.', 'Definition (1M) + EcoRI sequence (1M)', NULL, 'A base sequence reading identically on both antiparallel strands in the 5'' -> 3'' direction: 5''-GAATTC-3'' / 3''-CTTAAG-5''.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000068', 'List the four essential features required in an effective Cloning Vector like pBR322.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 2, '1. Origin of replication (ori — controls copy number), 2. Selectable marker (amp^R/tet^R or lacZ blue-white), 3. Single recognition/cloning sites for restriction enzymes, 4. Appropriate vector size.', '0.5M × 4', NULL, '1. Origin of replication (ori — controls copy number), 2. Selectable marker (amp^R/tet^R or lacZ blue-white), 3. Single recognition/cloning sites for restriction enzymes, 4. Appropriate vector size.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000069', 'Explain why Blue-White Screening (chromogenic X-gal substrate + β-galactosidase insertional inactivation) is preferred over two-antibiotic replica plating.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 3, 'Does not require cumbersome double plating on two antibiotics: non-recombinants express intact β-galactosidase turning X-gal BLUE, while recombinants with inserted DNA within lacZ gene lack enzyme and produce WHITE colonies directly.', '3M', NULL, 'Does not require cumbersome double plating on two antibiotics: non-recombinants express intact β-galactosidase turning X-gal BLUE, while recombinants with inserted DNA within lacZ gene lack enzyme and produce WHITE colonies directly.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006a', 'Describe how E. coli host cells are made "Competent" to take up hydrophilic recombinant plasmid DNA via Ca²⁺ and heat-shock.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 3, 'Treat cells with cold divalent Ca²⁺ ions increasing cell-wall pore uptake -> incubate on ice -> 42°C heat shock briefly -> place back on ice enabling plasmid entry.', '3M', NULL, 'Treat cells with cold divalent Ca²⁺ ions increasing cell-wall pore uptake -> incubate on ice -> 42°C heat shock briefly -> place back on ice enabling plasmid entry.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006b', 'Trace all three steps of Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) — Denaturation (94°C), Annealing (50–60°C primers), Extension (72°C Taq polymerase + dNTPs) — showing ~1 billion-fold amplification in 30 cycles. Also draw/explain a Stirred-Tank Bioreactor vs Sparged Stirred-Tank Bioreactor and explain Downstream Processing.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 5, 'PCR 3 steps + primers/Taq (3M) + Bioreactor agitation/O₂/pH/foam control + Downstream purification & clinical quality testing (2M).', 'PCR (3M) + Bioreactor & Downstream (2M)', NULL, 'PCR 3 steps + primers/Taq (3M) + Bioreactor agitation/O₂/pH/foam control + Downstream purification & clinical quality testing (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006c', '(i) First recombinant DNA molecule built by Stanley Cohen & Herbert Boyer (1972) used plasmid of which bacterium? (ii) Define "Elution" after agarose gel electrophoresis. (iii) Role of "rop" gene in pBR322. (iv) Why is Agrobacterium tumefaciens called "nature''s genetic engineer" and how is its Ti plasmid disarmed?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Principles and Processes', 4, '(i) Salmonella typhimurium antibiotic-resistance plasmid transferred into E. coli. (ii) Cutting separated DNA band piece from agarose gel and extracting pure DNA. (iii) Codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication. (iv) Naturally delivers T-DNA transforming dicot plants into crown-gall tumours; removing tumour-inducing genes leaves harmless cloning vector.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A biotech startup amplifies a rare patient gene fragment using 30 PCR cycles, isolates the desired band on an agarose gel, and scales recombinant enzyme production in 1,000-litre bioreactors.', '(i) Salmonella typhimurium antibiotic-resistance plasmid transferred into E. coli. (ii) Cutting separated DNA band piece from agarose gel and extracting pure DNA. (iii) Codes for proteins involved in plasmid replication. (iv) Naturally delivers T-DNA transforming dicot plants into crown-gall tumours; removing tumour-inducing genes leaves harmless cloning vector.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006d', 'Crystal protein (CryIAc / CryIIAb) produced by Bacillus thuringiensis activates inside the insect pest''s:', '{"A":"Acidic stomach","B":"Alkaline midgut pH (solubilising protoxin into active toxin poking midgut pores, killing insect)","C":"Salivary glands","D":"Nerve cord"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 1, 'Humans have acidic stomach -> Cry protoxin stays harmless!', NULL, NULL, 'Humans have acidic stomach -> Cry protoxin stays harmless!', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006e', 'RNA interference (RNAi) protects tobacco roots against nematode Meloidogyne incognita by using:', '{"A":"Antibiotics","B":"Agrobacterium introducing nematode-specific dsRNA (silencing mRNA translation)","C":"Bt spray","D":"Pesticides"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 1, 'Eukaryotic cellular defence mechanism silences complementary mRNA.', NULL, NULL, 'Eukaryotic cellular defence mechanism silences complementary mRNA.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006f', 'First clinical Gene Therapy (1990) cured a 4-year-old girl suffering from:', '{"A":"Diabetes","B":"ADA (Adenosine Deaminase) deficiency","C":"Cystic fibrosis","D":"Haemophilia"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 1, 'Functional ADA cDNA inserted into patient lymphocytes via retroviral vector.', NULL, NULL, 'Functional ADA cDNA inserted into patient lymphocytes via retroviral vector.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000070', 'Why couldn''t Eli Lilly manufacture mature human insulin directly from E. coli until 1983?', '{"A":"E. coli lacked ribosomes","B":"Pro-insulin contains an extra C-peptide cleaved during maturation; Eli Lilly synthesised A & B chains separately in E. coli and linked them via disulphide bonds","C":"A chain was toxic","D":"Insulin is lipid"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 1, 'Two separate plasmids made A and B chains linked by -S-S- bridges.', NULL, NULL, 'Two separate plasmids made A and B chains linked by -S-S- bridges.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000071', 'Use of bio-resources by multinational corporations without proper authorisation and compensatory sharing (e.g., Basmati rice, Turmeric, Neem disputes) is:', '{"A":"Bioremediation","B":"Biopiracy","C":"Biofortification","D":"Biomagnification"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 1, 'GEAC & Indian Patents Amendment safeguard traditional bio-heritage.', NULL, NULL, 'GEAC & Indian Patents Amendment safeguard traditional bio-heritage.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000072', 'Assertion (A): Golden Rice is nutritionally enriched with β-carotene (Vitamin A precursor) to combat childhood night blindness.
Reason (R): Genes encoding carotenoid biosynthesis enzymes were introduced into Oryza sativa endosperm.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 1, 'Classic transgenic biofortification crop.', NULL, NULL, 'Classic transgenic biofortification crop.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000073', 'How do CryIAb and CryIAc/CryIIAb specifically target Corn Borer vs Cotton Bollworms?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 2, 'cryIAc and cryIIAb control Cotton bollworms; cryIAb controls Corn borer.', '1M each', NULL, 'cryIAc and cryIIAb control Cotton bollworms; cryIAb controls Corn borer.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000074', 'Distinguish between ELISA, PCR, and Autoradiography in early disease diagnosis.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 2, 'PCR amplifies trace pathogen DNA before symptoms appear; ELISA detects antigen-antibody reaction; Autoradiography probes mutated non-binding single-stranded DNA bands.', '2M', NULL, 'PCR amplifies trace pathogen DNA before symptoms appear; ELISA detects antigen-antibody reaction; Autoradiography probes mutated non-binding single-stranded DNA bands.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000075', 'Explain the molecular mechanism of RNA Interference (RNAi) in creating pest-resistant Tobacco plants against Meloidogyne incognita.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 3, 'Nematode-specific genes introduced via Agrobacterium produce BOTH sense and anti-sense RNA inside host plant cells -> complementary RNAs form double-stranded dsRNA -> Dicer/RISC cleaves parasite mRNA blocking protein synthesis -> nematode cannot survive in transgenic root.', 'Sense/anti-sense dsRNA (1.5M) + Silencing & death (1.5M)', NULL, 'Nematode-specific genes introduced via Agrobacterium produce BOTH sense and anti-sense RNA inside host plant cells -> complementary RNAs form double-stranded dsRNA -> Dicer/RISC cleaves parasite mRNA blocking protein synthesis -> nematode cannot survive in transgenic root.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000076', 'State three vital roles played by Transgenic Animals (e.g., Rosie the transgenic cow) in medicine and biological safety testing.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 3, '1. Study normal physiology & disease models (cancer, cystic fibrosis, Alzheimer''s). 2. Produce biological products cleanly (α-1-antitrypsin for emphysema, Rosie cow milk with human α-lactalbumin 2.4 g/L). 3. Vaccine safety (polio mice) and accelerated chemical toxicity testing.', '3 × 1M', NULL, '1. Study normal physiology & disease models (cancer, cystic fibrosis, Alzheimer''s). 2. Produce biological products cleanly (α-1-antitrypsin for emphysema, Rosie cow milk with human α-lactalbumin 2.4 g/L). 3. Vaccine safety (polio mice) and accelerated chemical toxicity testing.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000077', 'Explain how Eli Lilly engineered recombinant human Insulin ("Humulin") overcoming the C-peptide bottleneck of pro-insulin. Also describe the 1990 ADA deficiency Gene Therapy protocol explaining why lymphocyte infusions require periodic repetition unless marrow stem cells are engineered permanently at early embryonic stage.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 5, 'Pro-insulin A, B, C structure + E. coli separate A/B synthesis & disulphide linkage (2.5M) + ADA lymphocyte vs early marrow stem-cell permanent cure (2.5M).', 'Humulin engineering (2.5M) + ADA Gene Therapy (2.5M)', NULL, 'Pro-insulin A, B, C structure + E. coli separate A/B synthesis & disulphide linkage (2.5M) + ADA lymphocyte vs early marrow stem-cell permanent cure (2.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000078', '(i) Expand GEAC and state its two regulatory mandates in India. (ii) What sparked India''s objection to the 1997 US Basmati rice patent? (iii) Mention three agricultural benefits of Genetically Modified (GM) crops beyond pest resistance. (iv) Contrast Insulin extracted from slaughtered pigs/cattle vs Humulin.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology and its Applications', 4, '(i) Genetic Engineering Approval Committee: validity of GM research & safety of introducing GM organisms for public services. (ii) Crossed Indian Basmati with semi-dwarf varieties claiming invention of traditional aroma/grain diversity (27 documented Basmati varieties exist in India). (iii) Abiotic stress tolerance (cold/drought/salt), reduced post-harvest loss, enhanced nutritional value (Golden Rice). (iv) Animal insulin triggered allergies in some patients; Humulin matches human insulin sequence avoiding immune rejection.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Agricultural regulators review Bt cotton field trials and biofortified rice strains alongside enforcing national bio-patent protections against unauthorised commercial biopiracy.', '(i) Genetic Engineering Approval Committee: validity of GM research & safety of introducing GM organisms for public services. (ii) Crossed Indian Basmati with semi-dwarf varieties claiming invention of traditional aroma/grain diversity (27 documented Basmati varieties exist in India). (iii) Abiotic stress tolerance (cold/drought/salt), reduced post-harvest loss, enhanced nutritional value (Golden Rice). (iv) Animal insulin triggered allergies in some patients; Humulin matches human insulin sequence avoiding immune rejection.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000079', 'In Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth curve dN/dt = rN[(K - N)/K], what does K represent?', '{"A":"Intrinsic rate of natural increase","B":"Carrying capacity (maximum population size environment can sustain)","C":"Mortality rate","D":"Lag phase"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 1, 'When N -> K, growth dN/dt -> 0 giving S-shaped sigmoid curve.', NULL, NULL, 'When N -> K, growth dN/dt -> 0 giving S-shaped sigmoid curve.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a', 'Brood parasitism where Koel (cuckoo) lays its eggs inside a crow''s nest whose eggs look astonishingly similar is an example of:', '{"A":"Mutualism","B":"Parasitism (co-evolved egg mimicry)","C":"Commensalism","D":"Amensalism"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 1, 'Host crow incubates cuckoo eggs unaware.', NULL, NULL, 'Host crow incubates cuckoo eggs unaware.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b', 'Cattle egret foraging beside grazing cattle, Barnacles on a whale''s back, and Orchids growing on a mango branch all exemplify:', '{"A":"Mutualism (+/+)","B":"Commensalism (+/0)","C":"Amensalism (-/0)","D":"Competition (-/-)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 1, 'One benefits while the other host is neither harmed nor benefited.', NULL, NULL, 'One benefits while the other host is neither harmed nor benefited.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c', 'Gause''s "Competitive Exclusion Principle" states that two closely related species competing for the SAME limiting resource:', '{"A":"Always merge","B":"Cannot co-exist indefinitely; the competitively inferior species is eventually eliminated (unless resource partitioning evolves)","C":"Double populations","D":"Become mutualists"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 1, 'MacArthur showed 5 warbler species co-existed via behavioural foraging partitioning.', NULL, NULL, 'MacArthur showed 5 warbler species co-existed via behavioural foraging partitioning.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d', 'Population equation N_{t+1} = N_t + [(B + I) - (D + E)] increases only if:', '{"A":"D + E > B + I","B":"Natality + Immigration (B + I) > Mortality + Emigration (D + E)","C":"Mortality = Natality","D":"Zero immigration"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 1, 'B + I add individuals; D + E remove individuals.', NULL, NULL, 'B + I add individuals; D + E remove individuals.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e', 'Assertion (A): The Mediterranean orchid Ophrys uses "sexual deceit" to ensure pollination by a specific bumblebee species.
Reason (R): One petal of Ophrys closely resembles the female bee in size, colour, and markings, prompting male bees to "pseudocopulate" and transfer pollen.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 1, 'Co-evolution locks orchid petal geometry to female bee form.', NULL, NULL, 'Co-evolution locks orchid petal geometry to female bee form.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007f', 'Sketch and contrast Exponential J-shaped growth (dN/dt = rN) vs Logistic S-shaped growth equations.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 2, 'Exponential: unlimited resources -> J-curve N_t = N₀ e^(rt). Logistic: finite resources with Carrying Capacity K -> S-curve dN/dt = rN(K-N)/K.', '1M each', NULL, 'Exponential: unlimited resources -> J-curve N_t = N₀ e^(rt). Logistic: finite resources with Carrying Capacity K -> S-curve dN/dt = rN(K-N)/K.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000080', 'How do Barnacles Balanus and Chathamalus on Scottish rocky coasts demonstrate Competitive Release (Connell''s field experiment)?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 2, 'Superior competitor Balanus dominates intertidal area excluding smaller Chathamalus; removing Balanus lets Chathamalus expand dramatically.', '2M', NULL, 'Superior competitor Balanus dominates intertidal area excluding smaller Chathamalus; removing Balanus lets Chathamalus expand dramatically.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000081', 'Construct an Age Pyramid comparison table for Expanding, Stable, and Declining human populations.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 3, 'Expanding: broad pre-reproductive base -> rapid growth (triangular). Stable: nearly equal pre-reproductive & reproductive bars (bell-shaped). Declining: narrow base < reproductive bar (urn-shaped).', '1M × 3', NULL, 'Expanding: broad pre-reproductive base -> rapid growth (triangular). Stable: nearly equal pre-reproductive & reproductive bars (bell-shaped). Declining: narrow base < reproductive bar (urn-shaped).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000082', 'Explain three plant defences (morphological & chemical) evolved against herbivory, citing Acacia/Cactus and Calotropis.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 3, 'Morphological: thorns (Acacia, Cactus). Chemical: toxic cardiac glycosides in Calotropis (Madar) repelling cattle/goats, nicotine/caffeine/quinine/strychnine/opium.', '1.5M + 1.5M', NULL, 'Morphological: thorns (Acacia, Cactus). Chemical: toxic cardiac glycosides in Calotropis (Madar) repelling cattle/goats, nicotine/caffeine/quinine/strychnine/opium.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000083', 'Compare all six Population Interactions (+/+, +/-, -/-, +/0, -/0) — Mutualism, Competition, Predation, Parasitism, Commensalism, and Amensalism — giving ONE exact NCERT ecological example for each (e.g., Mycorrhizae/Fig-Wasp, Abingdon tortoise vs goats, Prickly pear & Cactoblastis, Cuscuta/Liver fluke, Orchid-Mango, Penicillium/Antibiosis). Also explain why Predators are prudent ("never overexploit prey").', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 5, '6 interactions with signs & NCERT examples (4M) + Prudent predation & species diversity maintenance (Pisaster starfish experiment) (1M).', '6 interactions (4M) + Prudent predation (1M)', NULL, '6 interactions with signs & NCERT examples (4M) + Prudent predation & species diversity maintenance (Pisaster starfish experiment) (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000084', '(i) Define r (intrinsic rate of natural increase) for Norway rat (0.015) vs Flour beetle (0.12). (ii) Which organisms breed only once in a lifetime (Pacific salmon fish, Bamboo) vs multiple times? (iii) Explain Fig–Wasp obligate mutualism. (iv) Contrast Ectoparasite vs Endoparasite adaptations.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Organisms and Populations', 4, '(i) r = (b - d) measuring reproductive biotic potential; flour beetle grows faster. (ii) Semelparous once-breeders vs Iteroparous multiple-breeders (most birds/mammals). (iii) Wasp pollinates fig inflorescence while laying eggs inside developing fruits & larvae feed on few seeds. (iv) Loss of unnecessary sense organs/digestive system + adhesive hooks/suckers + high reproductive capacity.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Marine ecologists removing predatory starfish Pisaster from intertidal Pacific rocks watched 10 invertebrate species go extinct within a year due to unchecked interspecific competition.', '(i) r = (b - d) measuring reproductive biotic potential; flour beetle grows faster. (ii) Semelparous once-breeders vs Iteroparous multiple-breeders (most birds/mammals). (iii) Wasp pollinates fig inflorescence while laying eggs inside developing fruits & larvae feed on few seeds. (iv) Loss of unnecessary sense organs/digestive system + adhesive hooks/suckers + high reproductive capacity.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000085', 'Relationship between Gross Primary Productivity (GPP), Net Primary Productivity (NPP), and Respiration losses (R) is:', '{"A":"GPP = NPP - R","B":"NPP = GPP - R","C":"R = GPP + NPP","D":"NPP = GPP × R"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 1, 'NPP is the available biomass for heterotrophic consumers and decomposers.', NULL, NULL, 'NPP is the available biomass for heterotrophic consumers and decomposers.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000086', 'Correct sequence of the five steps of Decomposition in detritus is:', '{"A":"Catabolism -> Fragmentation -> Leaching -> Humification -> Mineralisation","B":"Fragmentation -> Leaching -> Catabolism -> Humification -> Mineralisation","C":"Leaching -> Mineralisation -> Fragmentation","D":"Humification -> Fragmentation -> Catabolism"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 1, 'Detritivores fragment -> water leaches -> microbial enzymes catabolise -> humus forms -> inorganic minerals release.', NULL, NULL, 'Detritivores fragment -> water leaches -> microbial enzymes catabolise -> humus forms -> inorganic minerals release.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000087', 'Which Ecological Pyramid is ALWAYS upright without exception in every natural ecosystem?', '{"A":"Pyramid of Numbers","B":"Pyramid of Biomass","C":"Pyramid of Energy (always 10% flow decreases upwards)","D":"All pyramids"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 1, 'Heat loss at every trophic transfer guarantees Pyramid of Energy is strictly upright.', NULL, NULL, 'Heat loss at every trophic transfer guarantees Pyramid of Energy is strictly upright.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000088', 'In a marine sea ecosystem, the Pyramid of Biomass is often:', '{"A":"Upright","B":"Inverted (small standing crop of fast-reproducing phytoplankton supports larger zooplankton/fish biomass)","C":"Spindle shaped","D":"Linear"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 1, 'High turnover rate lets tiny phytoplankton biomass feed heavier fish.', NULL, NULL, 'High turnover rate lets tiny phytoplankton biomass feed heavier fish.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000089', 'Percentage of Photosynthetically Active Radiation (PAR, <50% of incident sunlight) actually captured by green plants is:', '{"A":"50%","B":"2 – 10% of PAR (or ~1–5% of total solar radiation)","C":"25%","D":"0.01%"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 1, 'Only 2–10% of PAR drives the entire living world.', NULL, NULL, 'Only 2–10% of PAR drives the entire living world.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008a', 'Assertion (A): Decomposition rate slows drastically in cold anaerobic waterlogged bogs or when detritus is rich in lignin and chitin.
Reason (R): Warm, moist aerobic conditions and nitrogen/water-soluble sugar-rich detritus accelerate microbial decomposer activity.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 1, 'Chemical composition & microclimate govern breakdown speed.', NULL, NULL, 'Chemical composition & microclimate govern breakdown speed.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008b', 'Define Standing Crop and Standing State in an ecosystem.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 2, 'Standing crop: mass of living material (biomass or count) at a particular trophic level at a given time. Standing state: amount of inorganic nutrients (C, N, P, Ca) present in soil at any given time.', '1M each', NULL, 'Standing crop: mass of living material (biomass or count) at a particular trophic level at a given time. Standing state: amount of inorganic nutrients (C, N, P, Ca) present in soil at any given time.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008c', 'Distinguish between Grazing Food Chain (GFC) and Detritus Food Chain (DFC).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 2, 'GFC begins with living green plants (sunlight -> grass -> goat -> man). DFC begins with dead organic matter -> decomposers/detritivores (major energy conduit in terrestrial ecosystems).', '1M each', NULL, 'GFC begins with living green plants (sunlight -> grass -> goat -> man). DFC begins with dead organic matter -> decomposers/detritivores (major energy conduit in terrestrial ecosystems).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008d', 'Sketch and explain one Inverted Pyramid of Numbers (single large tree supporting birds & hyperparasites) and one Inverted Pyramid of Biomass (sea phytoplankton vs fishes).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 3, 'Tree pyramid (spindle/inverted count: 1 tree -> many insects/birds -> more parasites) + Sea biomass (small g/m² phytoplankton vs larger fish g/m²).', '1.5M each', NULL, 'Tree pyramid (spindle/inverted count: 1 tree -> many insects/birds -> more parasites) + Sea biomass (small g/m² phytoplankton vs larger fish g/m²).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008e', 'Explain Humification and Mineralisation during soil organic matter breakdown. State two properties of Humus.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 3, 'Humification forms dark amorphous colloidal Humus highly resistant to microbial action, decomposing very slowly as a nutrient reservoir. Mineralisation further degrades humus releasing inorganic minerals.', 'Definitions (2M) + 2 humus traits (1M)', NULL, 'Humification forms dark amorphous colloidal Humus highly resistant to microbial action, decomposing very slowly as a nutrient reservoir. Mineralisation further degrades humus releasing inorganic minerals.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008f', 'Define Primary Productivity (GPP vs NPP) and Secondary Productivity. Explain the five steps of Decomposition and four abiotic/biotic factors affecting Primary Productivity & Decomposition rates across Tropical rainforests vs Arctic tundra.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 5, 'Productivity definitions (2M) + 5 Decomposition steps (2M) + Climatic/substrate factors controlling rainforest vs ocean/desert rates (1M).', 'Productivity (2M) + Decomposition (2M) + Controlling factors (1M)', NULL, 'Productivity definitions (2M) + 5 Decomposition steps (2M) + Climatic/substrate factors controlling rainforest vs ocean/desert rates (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000090', '(i) Why are Food Webs more realistic than isolated linear Food Chains? (ii) Two limitations of Ecological Pyramids. (iii) Why is ocean primary productivity (~55 billion tons) lower than land (~115 billion tons) despite 70% surface cover? (iv) Calculate energy reaching 4th trophic level if producers hold 5,000 J.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 4, '(i) Organisms occupy >1 trophic level simultaneously (e.g., sparrow eats both seeds & insects). (ii) Ignores species at multiple trophic levels, assumes simple food chains, and excludes Saprophytes/Decomposers completely. (iii) Deep ocean lacks sunlight & mineral nutrients (nitrogen/iron limits). (iv) 5000 -> 500 -> 50 -> 5 J.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Marine ecologists compare deep pelagic ocean productivity against tropical rainforest canopies using satellite PAR estimates and 10% trophic transfer pyramids.', '(i) Organisms occupy >1 trophic level simultaneously (e.g., sparrow eats both seeds & insects). (ii) Ignores species at multiple trophic levels, assumes simple food chains, and excludes Saprophytes/Decomposers completely. (iii) Deep ocean lacks sunlight & mineral nutrients (nitrogen/iron limits). (iv) 5000 -> 500 -> 50 -> 5 J.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000091', 'Total global species diversity estimated conservatively by Robert May is roughly:', '{"A":"1.5 million","B":"About 7 million","C":"50 million","D":"100,000"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 1, 'Over 70% of recorded species are animals, of which ~70% are insects.', NULL, NULL, 'Over 70% of recorded species are animals, of which ~70% are insects.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000092', 'Species-Area relationship by Alexander von Humboldt on a log-log plot gives a straight line: log S = log C + Z log A. For frugivorous birds and mammals in tropical forests across continents, Z reaches:', '{"A":"0.1 to 0.2","B":"0.6 to 1.2 (specifically ~1.15, much steeper slope)","C":"0.01","D":"5.0"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 1, 'Smaller regions show Z = 0.1–0.2; entire continents show steeper Z = 0.6–1.2.', NULL, NULL, 'Smaller regions show Z = 0.1–0.2; entire continents show steeper Z = 0.6–1.2.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000093', '"Rivet Popper Hypothesis" illustrating how species extinction weakens an ecosystem like popping rivets off an aeroplane wing was proposed by:', '{"A":"Edward Wilson","B":"Paul Ehrlich","C":"David Tilman","D":"Norman Myers"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 1, 'Losing "key species" (wing rivets) causes rapid catastrophic collapse.', NULL, NULL, 'Losing "key species" (wing rivets) causes rapid catastrophic collapse.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000094', 'Most serious cause of accelerated species extinction among "The Evil Quartet" is:', '{"A":"Over-exploitation","B":"Habitat loss and fragmentation (e.g., Amazon rainforest shrinking from 14% to <6%)","C":"Alien species invasions","D":"Co-extinctions"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 1, 'Destruction of tropical rainforests & fragmenting large habitats leads all losses.', NULL, NULL, 'Destruction of tropical rainforests & fragmenting large habitats leads all losses.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000095', 'Which conservation approach protects whole ecosystems including India''s 14 Biosphere Reserves, 90 National Parks, and 448+ Wildlife Sanctuaries?', '{"A":"Ex-situ conservation","B":"In-situ (on-site) conservation and Biodiversity Hotspots","C":"Cryopreservation","D":"Seed banks"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 1, 'Protecting tigers saves the entire forest habitat simultaneously.', NULL, NULL, 'Protecting tigers saves the entire forest habitat simultaneously.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000096', 'Assertion (A): India holds only 2.4% of world''s land area yet contributes an impressive ~8.1% of global species diversity.
Reason (R): India spans 10 diverse biogeographical realms and includes 3 of the world''s 34 (36) Biodiversity Hotspots: Western Ghats-Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma, and Himalaya.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 1, 'High ecosystem & latitudinal variety makes India one of 12 megadiversity nations.', NULL, NULL, 'High ecosystem & latitudinal variety makes India one of 12 megadiversity nations.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000097', 'Distinguish between Genetic diversity, Species diversity, and Ecological diversity with one Indian example each.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 2, 'Genetic: 50,000 rice strains / 1000 mango varieties / Rauwolfia vomitoria reserpine potency. Species: Western Ghats amphibian richness > Eastern Ghats. Ecological: deserts, rainforests, mangroves, coral reefs, alpine meadows.', '2M', NULL, 'Genetic: 50,000 rice strains / 1000 mango varieties / Rauwolfia vomitoria reserpine potency. Species: Western Ghats amphibian richness > Eastern Ghats. Ecological: deserts, rainforests, mangroves, coral reefs, alpine meadows.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000098', 'Give three reasons why tropical latitudes (23.5°N to 23.5°S) harbour far greater biodiversity than temperate regions.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 2, '1. Undisturbed millions of years without frequent ice-age glaciations. 2. Less seasonal, constant predictable environments promoting niche specialisation. 3. More intense solar energy raising productivity.', '2M (any 2–3 reasons)', NULL, '1. Undisturbed millions of years without frequent ice-age glaciations. 2. Less seasonal, constant predictable environments promoting niche specialisation. 3. More intense solar energy raising productivity.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-000000000099', 'Explain the three categories of arguments for conserving biodiversity: Narrowly utilitarian, Broadly utilitarian, and Ethical.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 3, 'Narrowly utilitarian: direct food, firewood, fibres, medicines (~25% drugs plant-derived, taxol/morphine). Broadly utilitarian: ecosystem services like Amazon producing ~20% Earth O₂, bees pollination, flood control. Ethical: moral duty toward millions of co-inhabiting species.', '1M × 3', NULL, 'Narrowly utilitarian: direct food, firewood, fibres, medicines (~25% drugs plant-derived, taxol/morphine). Broadly utilitarian: ecosystem services like Amazon producing ~20% Earth O₂, bees pollination, flood control. Ethical: moral duty toward millions of co-inhabiting species.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009a', 'Give three examples of Alien Species Invasions and one example of Co-extinction causing native collapses.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 3, 'Nile perch in Lake Victoria wiped out 200+ cichlid fish; Water hyacinth (Eichhornia), Lantana, Parthenium choked Indian wetlands/soils; African catfish Clarias gariepinus threatens indigenous Indian catfishes. Co-extinction: host fish & unique parasites or fig & wasp partner.', '3 examples + Co-extinction (3M)', NULL, 'Nile perch in Lake Victoria wiped out 200+ cichlid fish; Water hyacinth (Eichhornia), Lantana, Parthenium choked Indian wetlands/soils; African catfish Clarias gariepinus threatens indigenous Indian catfishes. Co-extinction: host fish & unique parasites or fig & wasp partner.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009b', 'Explain "The Evil Quartet" — all four major causes driving global biodiversity loss with concrete examples (Passenger pigeon/Steller''s sea cow, Amazon lungs, Nile perch/Eichhornia, Parasite-host co-extinction). Contrast In-situ Conservation (Hotspots, Sacred Groves like Khasi/Jaintia Hills, Aravalli, Western Ghats) vs Ex-situ Conservation (Zoological parks, Botanical gardens, Cryopreservation in liquid N₂ at -196°C, Seed banks).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 5, 'Evil Quartet with 4 examples (3M) + In-situ vs Ex-situ with Hotspots & Cryopreservation (2M).', 'Evil Quartet (3M) + Conservation strategies (2M)', NULL, 'Evil Quartet with 4 examples (3M) + In-situ vs Ex-situ with Hotspots & Cryopreservation (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009c', '(i) Name two international summits (1992 Rio & 2002 Johannesburg) committing nations to curb biodiversity loss. (ii) What are the criteria for declaring a region a Biodiversity Hotspot? (iii) Number of global hotspots and the three covering India. (iv) David Tilman''s outdoor plot experiment finding linking species richness to stability.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity and Conservation', 4, '(i) Earth Summit (Rio de Janeiro 1992) and World Summit on Sustainable Development (Johannesburg 2002). (ii) Very high levels of species richness AND high degree of Endemism + accelerated habitat threat. (iii) 34 (originally 25+9); Western Ghats & Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma, and Himalaya. (iv) Plots with more species showed less year-to-year variation in total biomass and higher productivity.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Conservation biologists map endemic amphibian niches across Western Ghats shola forests alongside storing rare orthodox and recalcitrant seeds in liquid-nitrogen cryobanks.', '(i) Earth Summit (Rio de Janeiro 1992) and World Summit on Sustainable Development (Johannesburg 2002). (ii) Very high levels of species richness AND high degree of Endemism + accelerated habitat threat. (iii) 34 (originally 25+9); Western Ghats & Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma, and Himalaya. (iv) Plots with more species showed less year-to-year variation in total biomass and higher productivity.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009d', 'In an anther, the innermost wall layer that nourishes the developing pollen grains and typically possesses dense cytoplasm and multiple nuclei is:', '{"A":"Epidermis","B":"Endothecium","C":"Middle layers","D":"Tapetum"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', 1, 'Option (D) is correct. Tapetum is the innermost nourishing layer whose cells are often multinucleate and rich in cytoplasm; endothecium aids dehiscence.', 'Identify Tapetum as nutritive innermost anther layer [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Pollen exine is made of sporopollenin (most resistant organic material known!). | Common Pitfall: Confusing Tapetum with Endothecium (dehiscence).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009e', 'Which hormone acts directly on Sertoli cells inside seminiferous tubules to stimulate secretion of factors that help in spermiogenesis?', '{"A":"LH (Luteinising Hormone)","B":"FSH (Follicle Stimulating Hormone)","C":"Oxytocin","D":"Progesterone"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. LH acts on Leydig (interstitial) cells to secrete androgens, whereas FSH acts on Sertoli cells to assist spermiogenesis.', 'FSH -> Sertoli cells; LH -> Leydig cells [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mnemonic: "L"H -> "L"eydig cells; "F"SH -> "S"ertoli cells. | Common Pitfall: Swapping LH and FSH targets.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009f', 'Cu ions released by intra-uterine devices like CuT and Multiload 375 prevent pregnancy primarily by:', '{"A":"Preventing ovulation directly","B":"Suppressing sperm motility and their fertilising capacity","C":"Blocking fallopian tubes surgically","D":"Inducing menstruation immediately"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Reproductive Health', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Copper-releasing IUDs increase phagocytosis of sperms within the uterus and Cu²⁺ ions suppress sperm motility and fertilising ability.', 'Suppress sperm motility & fertilising capacity [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Hormone-releasing IUDs (LNG-20, Progestasert) additionally make the uterus unsuitable for implantation and cervix hostile to sperms. | Common Pitfall: Writing that CuT prevents ovulation.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a0', 'In the lac operon of E. coli, which structural gene codes for the enzyme β-galactosidase that hydrolyses lactose into glucose and galactose?', '{"A":"lac i","B":"lac z","C":"lac y","D":"lac a"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. z gene codes for β-galactosidase, y gene codes for permease, a gene codes for transacetylase, and i gene codes for the repressor.', 'Identify lac z -> β-galactosidase [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Notice "i" in lac i stands for "inhibitor" (repressor), NOT "inducer"! | Common Pitfall: Thinking lac y codes for β-galactosidase.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a1', 'In a population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, the frequency of homozygous recessive genotype (aa) is 0.16. What is the frequency of heterozygous carriers (Aa)?', '{"A":"0.36","B":"0.48","C":"0.24","D":"0.60"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. q² = 0.16 => q = 0.4. Since p + q = 1, p = 0.6. Heterozygous frequency 2pq = 2 × 0.6 × 0.4 = 0.48 (48%).', 'q = 0.4, p = 0.6 -> 2pq = 0.48 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always verify p² + 2pq + q² = 0.36 + 0.48 + 0.16 = 1.00! | Common Pitfall: Forgetting the factor of 2 in 2pq.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a2', 'Colostrum secreted by the mother during initial days of lactation provides abundant IgA antibodies to the newborn. This is an example of:', '{"A":"Natural active immunity","B":"Natural passive immunity","C":"Artificial active immunity","D":"Cell-mediated immunity"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Human Health & Disease', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Pre-formed IgA antibodies are transferred naturally from mother to infant (no host antigen exposure/memory), making it natural passive immunity.', 'Pre-formed IgA transferred naturally -> Natural passive immunity [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Anti-venom / Tetanus antitoxin = Artificial passive; Vaccination = Artificial active; Colostrum / Placental IgG = Natural passive. | Common Pitfall: Marking active immunity.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a3', 'During secondary sewage treatment, "flocs" consist of:', '{"A":"Anaerobic methanogens only","B":"Masses of aerobic bacteria associated with fungal filaments forming mesh-like structures","C":"Heavy grit and floating plastic debris","D":"Pure nitrogen-fixing Rhizobium"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Microbes in Human Welfare', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Vigorous aeration in aeration tanks promotes growth of useful aerobic microbes into flocs (bacteria + fungal filaments) that consume organic matter and sharply lower BOD.', 'Aerobic bacteria + fungal filaments lowering BOD [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Higher BOD means MORE polluting potential of sewage water! | Common Pitfall: Confusing aerobic flocs with anaerobic sludge digester methanogens.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a4', 'Which enzyme is used to join sticky ends of vector DNA and foreign DNA fragments by forming phosphodiester bonds?', '{"A":"EcoRI","B":"Taq polymerase","C":"DNA ligase","D":"Exonuclease"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology Principles', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. DNA ligase (molecular glue) seals sugar-phosphate backbones via phosphodiester bonds to form recombinant DNA.', 'Identify DNA ligase [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: EcoRI = molecular scissors (cuts between G and A in 5''-GAATTC-3''); Taq polymerase = heat-stable PCR DNA synthesis; DNA ligase = molecular glue. | Common Pitfall: Selecting Taq polymerase.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a5', 'Why does the Bt toxin protein crystal produced by Bacillus thuringiensis NOT kill the bacterium itself?', '{"A":"Bacterium has a thick chitin wall","B":"Toxin exists as an inactive protoxin and activates only in the alkaline pH of the insect midgut","C":"Toxin only kills plant cells","D":"Acidic bacterial cytoplasm destroys it"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology Applications', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Inside Bacillus, Bt toxin exists as inactive protoxin; once ingested by an insect, the alkaline pH of the midgut solubilises the crystals and converts protoxin into active toxin that creates pores in midgut epithelial cells.', 'Inactive protoxin activated by alkaline insect gut pH [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Specify "alkaline pH of gut" + "pores in midgut epithelial cells causing swelling and lysis". | Common Pitfall: Writing "acidic" gut pH instead of "alkaline"!', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a6', 'In the Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth equation dN/dt = rN [(K - N)/K], when N equals K (carrying capacity), the population growth rate dN/dt becomes:', '{"A":"Maximum","B":"Equal to rN","C":"Zero (asymptote phase)","D":"Exponential"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Organisms & Populations', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. When N = K, (K - N)/K = 0, so dN/dt = 0 and population size stabilises at carrying capacity K (sigmoid S-shaped curve).', 'N = K => (K - N)/K = 0 => dN/dt = 0 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Sigmoid curve shows Lag -> Log/Acceleration -> Deceleration -> Asymptote. | Common Pitfall: Thinking dN/dt is maximum when N = K (max growth rate actually occurs at N = K/2!).', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a7', 'Which ecological pyramid can NEVER be inverted in any natural ecosystem?', '{"A":"Pyramid of biomass in sea","B":"Pyramid of number on a big tree","C":"Pyramid of energy","D":"Pyramid of biomass in grassland"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem & Biodiversity', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Pyramid of energy is ALWAYS upright because energy always flows unidirectionally from lower to higher trophic levels, with ~90% lost as heat at every step (10% law).', 'Pyramid of energy always upright due to thermodynamic heat loss [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Biomass pyramid in sea (phytoplankton < zooplankton/fishes) IS inverted! | Common Pitfall: Saying pyramid of biomass is always upright.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a8', 'Assertion (A): Rapid secretion of LH leading to its maximum level during the mid-cycle (day 14) induces rupture of Graafian follicle and ovulation.
Reason (R): High LH surge triggers degeneration of corpus luteum before fertilisation.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Reproduction', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Mid-cycle LH surge causes ovulation, after which the empty Graafian follicle transforms INTO the corpus luteum (it does not degenerate until day ~26–28 if fertilisation fails).', 'Option (C): A true, R false [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: LH surge -> Ovulation -> Remainder becomes Corpus Luteum secreting Progesterone. | Common Pitfall: Missing the false claim about corpus luteum degeneration at mid-cycle.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a9', 'Assertion (A): During DNA replication, synthesis on one template strand is continuous while on the other it is discontinuous (Okazaki fragments).
Reason (R): DNA-dependent DNA polymerase can polymerise nucleotides ONLY in the 5'' → 3'' direction.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Molecular Inheritance', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Because DNA polymerase only extends 3''-OH (synthesising 5''→3''), the 3''→5'' template is copied continuously while the 5''→3'' template must be copied discontinuously away from the replication fork.', 'Option (A) citing strict 5''→3'' polymerase polarity [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Draw the replication fork arrows in 2M/3M questions clearly. | Common Pitfall: Saying Okazaki fragments grow 3''→5''.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000aa', 'Assertion (A): During agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments move towards the positive anode under an electric field.
Reason (R): DNA molecules are negatively charged due to phosphate groups in their sugar-phosphate backbone.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Negatively charged DNA migrates toward the positive anode through agarose pores, with smaller fragments travelling farther.', 'Option (A) citing negative phosphate backbone charge [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Stained with Ethidium Bromide and viewed under UV light as bright orange bands! | Common Pitfall: Thinking larger fragments travel faster.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ab', 'Assertion (A): Biodiversity hotspots are examples of ex-situ conservation.
Reason (R): Hotspots exhibit very high levels of species richness and high degree of endemism under urgent threat of habitat loss.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity', 1, 'Option (D) is correct. Biodiversity hotspots (like Western Ghats & Sri Lanka, Indo-Burma, Himalaya) are prime examples of IN-SITU (on-site) conservation, not ex-situ. Thus A is false, R is true.', 'Option (D): Hotspots are in-situ conservation, so A false, R true [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: In-situ: Hotspots, Biosphere reserves, National parks, Sacred groves; Ex-situ: Seed banks, Botanical gardens, Cryopreservation. | Common Pitfall: Assuming hotspots are ex-situ.', 'EASY', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ac', 'Define Apomixis and Polyembryony. Why does the hybrid seed industry strongly desire transferring apomictic genes into hybrid crops?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Flowering Plants', 2, 'Apomixis: Production of seeds without fertilisation (asexual reproduction mimicking sexual reproduction, e.g., Asteraceae and grasses).
Polyembryony: Occurrence of more than one embryo in a single seed (e.g., Citrus, Mango nucellar cells).
Hybrid Advantage: Hybrid seeds lose vigour in F₂ due to segregation and cost farmers heavily every year; if made apomictic, there is no segregation of hybrid traits so farmers can replant saved seeds year after year.', 'Definitions of Apomixis & Polyembryony [1 Mark] | Preventing hybrid trait segregation for farmers [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Highlight "no segregation of characters in hybrid progeny". | Common Pitfall: Confusing apomixis (seed without fertilisation) with parthenocarpy (fruit without fertilisation -> seedless).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ad', 'A colour-blind man marries a woman with normal vision whose father was colour-blind. Work out a Punnett square to show the probability of their sons and daughters being colour-blind.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Inheritance', 2, 'Man = X^c Y; Woman = carrier X^C X^c (inherited X^c from colour-blind father).
Gametes: Man (X^c, Y) × Woman (X^C, X^c).
Offspring genotypes:
• X^C X^c (Carrier daughter) — 25%
• X^c X^c (Colour-blind daughter) — 25% (50% of daughters)
• X^C Y (Normal son) — 25%
• X^c Y (Colour-blind son) — 25% (50% of sons).
50% of sons and 50% of daughters will be colour-blind.', 'Parental genotypes X^c Y and carrier X^C X^c [1 Mark] | Punnett cross showing 50% sons & 50% daughters affected [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember a daughter is affected ONLY when father is affected AND mother is at least a carrier! | Common Pitfall: Assuming the woman is homozygous normal X^C X^C despite her colour-blind father.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ae', 'What are interferons, and under which category of innate immunity barriers are they classified?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Human Health', 2, 'Interferons are protective antiviral proteins secreted by virus-infected cells that protect neighbouring non-infected cells from further viral infection.
They belong to the Cytokine barriers of Innate Immunity.', 'Antiviral proteins from virus-infected cells [1 Mark] | Cytokine barrier of Innate Immunity [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Know all 4 NCERT Innate Barriers: Physical (skin/mucus), Physiological (HCl/tears), Cellular (PMNL/NK cells), Cytokine (Interferons). | Common Pitfall: Classifying interferons under Cellular barriers.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000af', 'Name the bioactive molecule and its source microbe used as:
(i) Immunosuppressive agent in organ-transplant patients
(ii) Blood-cholesterol lowering agent.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Microbes', 2, '(i) Cyclosporin A — produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum.
(ii) Statins — produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus (competitively inhibits enzyme responsible for cholesterol synthesis).', 'Cyclosporin A from Trichoderma polysporum [1 Mark] | Statins from Monascus purpureus [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Also remember Streptokinase ("clot buster") from bacterium Streptococcus. | Common Pitfall: Swapping Trichoderma polysporum and Monascus purpureus.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b0', 'List the four major causes of accelerated species extinction collectively termed "The Evil Quartet", explaining any one with an example.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Biology', 'Biodiversity', 2, 'The Evil Quartet:
1. Habitat loss and fragmentation (most important — e.g., Amazon rainforest cleared for soy/cattle).
2. Over-exploitation (e.g., Steller''s sea cow, Passenger pigeon).
3. Alien species invasions (e.g., Nile perch introduced into Lake Victoria killed 200+ native cichlid fish; Eichhornia/Lantana).
4. Co-extinctions (obligatory host-parasite or plant-pollinator mutualism extinction).', 'List all 4 Evil Quartet causes accurately [1 Mark] | Clear example explaining one cause [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Highlight Habitat Loss & Fragmentation as the single biggest driver. | Common Pitfall: Listing climate pollution without the exact 4 NCERT Evil Quartet terms.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b1', 'Why did Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase use radioactive isotopes ³²P and ³⁵S separately in bacteriophage infection experiments? State their final observation and conclusion.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Molecular Inheritance', 3, '• Rationale: DNA contains Phosphorus (³²P) but NO Sulphur, whereas Protein coat contains Sulphur (³⁵S, in methionine/cysteine) but NO Phosphorus.
• Procedure (3 steps): Infection of E. coli -> Blending (to agitate viral coats off bacteria) -> Centrifugation (heavier bacteria settle in pellet; lighter viral coats stay in supernatant).
• Observation & Conclusion: Bacteria infected with ³²P phages were radioactive in the PELLET, whereas ³⁵S radioactivity remained only in the SUPERNATANT. Proved conclusively that DNA (not protein) is the genetic material transferred from virus to bacteria.', '³²P labels DNA only; ³⁵S labels Protein only [1 Mark] | Infection, Blending, Centrifugation steps [1 Mark] | ³²P in pellet -> DNA is genetic material [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mention exact words: Blending and Centrifugation, Pellet vs Supernatant! | Common Pitfall: Saying ³⁵S entered the bacterial pellet.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b2', 'Differentiate between Homologous and Analogous organs with one plant and one animal example of each, naming the type of evolution they signify.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Evolution', 3, '1. Homologous Organs: Similar fundamental anatomical structure & origin, adapted for different functions (Divergent Evolution). Examples: Forelimbs of Whale, Bat, Cheetah, Human; Thorns of Bougainvillea and Tendrils of Cucurbita.
2. Analogous Organs: Different anatomical structure & origin, evolved for similar functions (Convergent Evolution). Examples: Wings of butterfly and bird (or Eye of octopus & mammals; Flippers of penguins & dolphins); Sweet potato (root modification) and Potato (stem modification).', 'Homologous = same origin, divergent evolution + 2 examples [1.5 Marks] | Analogous = different origin, convergent evolution + 2 examples [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always memorize ONE plant example and ONE animal example for both! | Common Pitfall: Swapping divergent (homologous) and convergent (analogous).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b3', 'Trace the key stages of HIV replication inside a human macrophage and explain why HIV-infected patients suffer severe opportunistic infections.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Human Health', 3, '1. HIV enters host Macrophage (acts as "HIV factory").
2. Viral RNA genome is reverse-transcribed into viral DNA by enzyme Reverse Transcriptase.
3. Viral DNA incorporates into host cell genome and directs synthesis of new viral RNA and particles.
4. Progeny HIV simultaneously enters Helper T-lymphocytes (T_H cells), replicates and destroys them, causing a progressive drop in T_H count. Lacking cell-mediated immunity, patients succumb to opportunistic infections (Mycobacterium, viruses, Toxoplasma).', 'Macrophage entry + Reverse Transcriptase (RNA -> DNA) [1.5 Marks] | Host integration + Helper T-cell depletion -> immunodeficiency [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always specify BOTH cell types: Macrophage ("HIV factory") and Helper T-lymphocytes (T_H drop)! | Common Pitfall: Saying HIV destroys B-cells directly instead of Helper T-cells.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b4', 'Describe the three sequential thermal steps of one Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) cycle with temperatures and the special DNA polymerase used.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology Principles', 3, 'Enzyme used: Thermostable DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase) isolated from Thermus aquaticus (remains active at 94°C).
1. Denaturation (~94°C): Double-stranded target DNA separates into two single strands.
2. Annealing (~50–60°C): Two short oligonucleotide primers hydrogen-bond to complementary 3'' ends of target strands.
3. Extension (~72°C): Taq polymerase adds dNTPs onto primers in 5''→3'' direction, doubling DNA copies per cycle (~1 billion copies in 30 cycles).', 'Denaturation, Annealing, Extension in order [2 Marks] | Taq polymerase from Thermus aquaticus & role [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Show both primers and 5''→3'' extension direction clearly. | Common Pitfall: Putting Annealing before Denaturation.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b5', 'Why is insulin extracted from slaughtered cattle/pigs less preferred today? Explain how Eli Lilly engineered recombinant human insulin (Humulin) in 1983 using E. coli.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology Applications', 3, 'Animal insulin triggered allergic immune reactions in some diabetic patients.
Pro-insulin in humans has three chains (A, B, and extra C-peptide) which is removed during maturation into active A–B linked by disulphide bridges.
Eli Lilly prepared two separate DNA sequences corresponding to Chain A and Chain B of human insulin, inserted them separately into plasmids of E. coli to produce separate A and B chains, extracted them, and combined them by forming disulphide bonds to assemble functional human insulin.', 'Allergy drawback + pro-insulin C-peptide removal challenge [1.5 Marks] | Separate A & B synthesis in E. coli + disulphide linkage [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Key detail: Chain A and Chain B were synthesised SEPARATELY and then joined via disulphide bonds! | Common Pitfall: Saying E. coli processed pro-insulin directly (prokaryotes lack splicing/processing machinery!).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b6', 'Differentiate between Mutualism, Commensalism, and Brood Parasitism, giving one specific NCERT example of each.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Organisms & Populations', 3, '1. Mutualism (+, +): Both species benefit. Example: Lichens (algae + fungus), Mycorrhizae, or Fig tree and Wasp pollinator (or Orchid Ophrys sexual deceit).
2. Commensalism (+, 0): One benefits while host is neither harmed nor benefited. Example: Orchid growing as epiphyte on mango branch, Barnacles on whale back, Cattle egret and grazing cattle.
3. Brood Parasitism: Parasitic bird lays eggs in host nest letting host incubate them (eggs evolve to mimic host egg colour/size). Example: Cuckoo (Koel) laying eggs in Crow''s nest.', 'Three definitions with (+/+), (+/0), (+/-) signs [1.5 Marks] | One exact NCERT example for each [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Give (+, +), (+, 0), (+, -) symbols cleanly in table or bullets. | Common Pitfall: Confusing Amensalism (-, 0) with Commensalism (+, 0).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b7', 'Explain the five sequential steps involved in the process of decomposition of detritus in a terrestrial ecosystem.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Biology', 'Ecosystem', 3, '1. Fragmentation: Detritivores (earthworms) break detritus into smaller particles.
2. Leaching: Water-soluble inorganic nutrients percolate into soil horizon and precipitate as unavailable salts.
3. Catabolism: Bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade detritus into simpler inorganic substances.
4. Humification: Accumulation of dark amorphous colloidal substance called humus (highly resistant to microbial action, decomposes very slowly, nutrient reservoir).
5. Mineralisation: Further degradation of humus by microbes releasing inorganic nutrients back into soil.', 'Fragmentation, Leaching, Catabolism [1.5 Marks] | Humification & Mineralisation explained [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember that Fragmentation, Leaching, and Catabolism occur SIMULTANEOUSLY on detritus. | Common Pitfall: Putting Mineralisation before Humification.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b8', 'Read the passage and answer:
Plasmids like pBR322 contain an origin of replication (ori), selectable markers (amp^R and tet^R), and unique cloning sites such as BamHI inside tet^R. Alternatively, chromogenic substrates (X-gal) allow blue-white screening using β-galactosidase.

(a) What is the function of the "ori" site in a cloning vector? (1M)
(b) If foreign DNA is ligated at the BamHI site of pBR322, how will you distinguish recombinant colonies from non-recombinant transformants? (2M)
(c) Why is blue-white colour selection preferred over antibiotic double-plating? (1M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) ori is the sequence from where replication starts, and also controls the copy number of the linked foreign DNA.
(b) Inserting DNA at BamHI inactivates the tet^R gene (insertional inactivation), while amp^R remains intact. Transformants first grow on ampicillin medium; transferring replicas onto tetracycline kills recombinants while non-recombinants grow on BOTH antibiotics.
(c) Double antibiotic plating is cumbersome (requires simultaneous plating on two media plates); chromogenic blue-white screening separates white recombinants from blue non-recombinants on a single plate.', '(a) Starts replication & controls copy number [1 Mark] | (b) tet^R inactivated -> grows on Amp, dies on Tet [2 Marks] | (c) Single-plate convenience vs cumbersome double plating [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Notice: Recombinants produce WHITE colonies (gene inactivated -> no enzyme) while non-recombinants turn BLUE! | Common Pitfall: Thinking recombinant colonies turn blue in β-galactosidase screening.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b9', 'Read the case and answer:
In a family pedigree chart, unaffected carrier parents give birth to an affected child (both boys and girls can be affected equally), whereas an affected individual rarely transmits the trait directly to the next generation if marrying an unaffected homozygous partner.

(a) Identify the mode of inheritance (Autosomal/Sex-linked; Dominant/Recessive) and give one classic human example. (2M)
(b) State the defect in Sickle-Cell Anaemia at the DNA/protein codon level. (1.5M)
(c) Why is Sickle-Cell Anaemia still maintained at noticeable frequency in malaria-endemic regions? (0.5M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Biology', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Autosomal Recessive inheritance (skips generations, affects males & females equally). Example: Sickle-cell anaemia or Phenylketonuria / Thalassemia.
(b) Point mutation in β-globin gene on chromosome 11: GAG mutates to GUG in mRNA, substituting Glutamic acid (Glu) with Valine (Val) at the 6th position of β-globin chain.
(c) Heterozygous carriers (Hb^A Hb^S) enjoy selective advantage against severe Plasmodium falciparum malaria.', '(a) Autosomal recessive + example [2 Marks] | (b) GAG -> GUG / 6th position Glu -> Val [1.5 Marks] | (c) Heterozygote malaria resistance [0.5 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Pedigree shortcut: Normal parents having affected child => ALWAYS Recessive! | Common Pitfall: Calling unaffected parents having affected child "Dominant".', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ba', '(a) Trace the sequence of stages of Oogenesis in human females, noting when Meiosis I and Meiosis II complete.
(b) Explain the hormonal changes (LH, FSH, Oestrogen, Progesterone) across the Follicular, Ovulatory, and Luteal phases of a 28-day menstrual cycle.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Human Reproduction', 5, '(a) Oogonia (2n, formed before birth!) enter Prophase I and arrest as Primary oocytes (2n). At puberty, primary follicles mature into tertiary follicles; Primary oocyte completes Meiosis I inside tertiary follicle forming large Secondary oocyte (n) and tiny First polar body (n). Meiosis II arrests at Metaphase II and completes ONLY upon sperm entry during fertilisation, forming Ovum (n) and Second polar body.
(b) • Follicular phase (days 5–13): FSH & LH rise gradually, stimulating follicular growth and Oestrogen secretion to regenerate endometrium.
• Ovulatory phase (day 14): Peak Oestrogen triggers sharp LH surge, rupturing Graafian follicle to release secondary oocyte.
• Luteal phase (days 15–28): Corpus luteum secretes high Progesterone to maintain thick secretory endometrium for implantation. Without fertilisation, corpus luteum regresses into corpus albicans, Progesterone falls, causing menstruation.', '(a) Foetal Prophase I arrest -> Meiosis I -> Metaphase II fertilisation arrest [2.5 Marks] | (b) FSH/LH/Oestrogen/Progesterone roles across all 3 phases [2.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Highlight BOTH meiotic arrest points in Oogenesis (Prophase I before birth; Metaphase II before fertilisation)! | Common Pitfall: Stating Meiosis II completes inside ovary before ovulation.', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bb', '(a) Draw a schematic labelled diagram of a Transcription Unit and explain why BOTH DNA strands are not copied during transcription.
(b) State four salient features of the Genetic Code.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', 5, '(a) Transcription unit components: Promoter (at 5'' end of coding strand), Structural gene, Terminator (at 3'' end of coding strand). Template strand runs 3''→5'' while Coding strand runs 5''→3'' (matching mRNA sequence except T→U).
Why not both strands: (1) Two complementary mRNAs would encode two completely different proteins, complicating genetic transfer; (2) Two complementary RNAs would pair to form double-stranded RNA (dsRNA), blocking translation into protein.
(b) Genetic Code features: (1) Triplet (61 sense codons + 3 stop codons UAA, UAG, UGA); (2) Unambiguous/Specific (1 codon = 1 amino acid); (3) Degenerate (multiple codons for most amino acids); (4) Universal across bacteria to humans & Commaless; (5) AUG is dual initiator (codes Methionine + start codon).', '(a) Promoter/Structural/Terminator polarity + 2 reasons against dual-strand copying [3 Marks] | (b) 4 clear features of Genetic Code [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Promoter position is ALWAYS referenced w.r.t. the 5'' end of the CODING strand! | Common Pitfall: Writing that Template strand sequence matches mRNA (it is complementary; CODING strand matches mRNA!).', 'HARD', true),
        ('12031111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bc', '(a) Explain how ADA (Adenosine Deaminase) deficiency was treated in a 4-year-old girl in 1990 using gene therapy. Why is periodic infusion of engineered lymphocytes required, and how can a permanent cure be achieved?
(b) State four ways transgenic animals are beneficial to human medicine and research.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Biology', 'Biotechnology Applications', 5, '(a) Lymphocytes from patient''s blood were cultured outside; a functional ADA cDNA was introduced into them using a retroviral vector, and engineered lymphocytes were returned to the patient. Because lymphocytes are NOT immortal, patients require periodic infusions. A permanent cure requires isolating the gene from marrow cells and introducing it at early EMBRYONIC stages.
(b) Transgenic animal benefits: (1) Studying normal physiology & gene regulation (e.g., insulin-like growth factor); (2) Disease models (cancer, cystic fibrosis, Alzheimer''s); (3) Biological products (α-1-antitrypsin for emphysema; Rosie cow producing 2.4 g/L human α-lactalbumin enriched milk); (4) Vaccine safety & chemical toxicity testing.', '(a) Retroviral ADA cDNA in lymphocytes + embryonic permanent cure [3 Marks] | (b) Four transgenic uses with Rosie / α-1-antitrypsin examples [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Cite exact year 1990, Rosie transgenic cow (1997, 2.4 g/L α-lactalbumin), and α-1-antitrypsin. | Common Pitfall: Saying lymphocyte infusion gives permanent cure.', 'HARD', true)
